import{t as e}from"./links-ClIw6rLF.js";var t={id:`F3`,title:`AC Electrical Fundamentals`,level:`beginner`,stage:`foundation`,summary:`Alternating quantities, RMS, reactance and impedance, AC power and power factor, harmonics, transformers and rectification.`,prerequisites:[`F2`],recommended:[],status:`published`,phase:8,competency:`CU-03`,modules:[{id:`F3.M1`,title:`Alternating quantities`,summary:`Why AC is used at all, and the vocabulary — amplitude, period, frequency, phase, peak, average and RMS — needed for everything else in this course.`,lessons:[{id:`F3.M1.L1`,title:`Why AC is used`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain why electrical power is generated, transmitted and mostly used as AC rather than DC.`,`Explain the role of the transformer in making AC practical for long-distance transmission.`,`State why an inverter must synthesise AC from a DC source, rather than simply being "turned on".`],introduction:`You have already met [[dc|DC]] and [[ac|AC]] in F1 as a safety distinction. This course picks the same two ideas up as an engineering one: WHY does almost every grid, and every inverter's output, use AC rather than DC? The short answer is **transformers** — and transformers only work on AC. Understanding this motivates everything else in F3: reactance, impedance, power factor, and the whole reason inverters exist.`,lecture:`### The transmission problem

Electrical power is P = V × I. For a given power delivered, a higher transmission voltage means a lower current, and lower current means smaller conductors and far less resistive loss (P_loss = I²R) over long transmission distances. So power is generated and transmitted at a HIGH voltage, then stepped down to a safe, usable LOW voltage close to where it is consumed.

### Why this needs AC

Stepping voltage up and down efficiently, at high power, with no moving parts and very little loss, is exactly what a **transformer** does — and a transformer works by mutual induction (F3.M4), which requires a CHANGING magnetic field. A steady DC current produces a steady, unchanging magnetic field and induces nothing in a second winding. An alternating current, by continuously changing, induces a continuously changing voltage in the other winding, scaled by the turns ratio. **No practical, lossless way to transform a DC voltage up and down existed until relatively recently (modern DC-DC converters, themselves often built around a high-frequency AC stage inside them) — AC transformers have been doing this job cheaply and reliably for over a century.**

### Why your appliances and the grid still use AC today

Having built an entire grid around efficient AC transmission and transformation, it then made sense to standardise AC at the point of use too: motors, lighting and most appliances are designed to run directly from the grid's AC supply.

### Why an inverter has to *synthesise* AC

A battery or solar array is fundamentally a DC source: it has a fixed polarity and does not reverse on its own. To supply AC loads (or feed an AC grid) from a DC source, an inverter must actively and repeatedly REVERSE the polarity presented to the output, at the required frequency (50 or 60 Hz) — this is exactly the job of the H-bridge you built in the Circuit Connection Laboratory. "Inverting" DC to AC is therefore a genuinely active process requiring switching devices and control, not a passive one.`,technical:`**DC transmission exists too.** High-Voltage DC (HVDC) transmission is used for some very long or undersea links, because it avoids some AC-specific losses (reactive power flow) over extreme distances; but it requires expensive power-electronic converter stations at each end to interface with the surrounding AC grid, so it remains the exception, not the rule, for ordinary transmission and distribution.

**Frequency choice (50 vs 60 Hz) is historical, not fundamental.** Different regions settled on 50 Hz or 60 Hz for historical reasons related to early generator and motor designs; neither is intrinsically "better", and this programme treats both as valid depending on the market an inverter is designed for.`,diagrams:[{id:`system-types`,caption:`Generation at high voltage, step-down by transformer toward the point of use, and the inverter's role in supplying AC loads from a DC source.`}],formulas:[{name:`Transmission loss`,expr:`P_loss = I² · R_line`,where:[[`P_loss`,`power lost in the transmission line`,`W`],[`I`,`line current`,`A`],[`R_line`,`the line's resistance`,`Ω`]],note:`For fixed power delivered, raising voltage lowers current and sharply lowers loss.`}],examples:[{title:`Why higher transmission voltage reduces loss`,problem:`A 100 kW load is supplied at (a) 1000 V and (b) 10,000 V, through a line of resistance 2 Ω. Compare the transmission loss.`,steps:[`(a) I = P/V = 100,000/1000 = 100 A; loss = I²R = 100² × 2 = 20,000 W`,`(b) I = 100,000/10,000 = 10 A; loss = 10² × 2 = 200 W`],answer:`Raising the voltage tenfold cuts the current tenfold and the transmission loss by a hundredfold (20 kW → 200 W) — the direct motivation for high-voltage transmission.`,check:`Loss scales with I², so a tenfold drop in current gives a hundredfold drop in loss, consistent with the result.`},{title:`Why a transformer cannot step down DC`,problem:`Explain, in terms of mutual induction, why connecting a DC source to a transformer's primary winding does not produce a stepped-down voltage at the secondary (beyond a brief instant at switch-on).`,steps:[`A transformer induces voltage in its secondary winding only from a CHANGING magnetic flux`,`A steady DC current produces a steady flux once established`,`With no ongoing change in flux, no ongoing voltage is induced`],answer:`Only at the instant of switching the DC on or off does the flux change, inducing a brief pulse; a steady DC current gives essentially zero secondary voltage.`}],applications:`This is the engineering reason the Build Your Own Inverter laboratory exists at all: converting a battery's or solar array's DC into usable AC is not optional plumbing, it is the entire function of the product.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`"AC is just how electricity naturally comes out of a generator; DC needs special equipment."`,correction:`Both require specific generator designs; AC was chosen deliberately because it pairs with transformers for efficient transmission.`},{mistake:`Assuming an inverter "converts" DC to AC the way a translator converts language, with no active switching involved.`,correction:`An inverter actively and repeatedly reverses the polarity presented to its output using switching devices under control — it is a genuinely active process.`},{mistake:`Thinking higher voltage is dangerous, so transmission always uses lower voltage.`,correction:`Transmission deliberately uses high voltage to minimise current and loss; the resulting safety management (clearances, insulation, access control) is handled separately, not avoided.`}],demo:{title:`Revisit the H-bridge`,text:`The Circuit Connection Laboratory's H-bridge exercise is the switching core behind every inverter output waveform this course studies.`,link:e.ckt(`H-bridge exercise`,`ckt-09`)},activities:[e.ckt(`Full H-bridge exercise`,`ckt-09`),e.lib(`Transformer`,`transformer`),e.fun(`Fundamentals: DC vs AC (fun-01)`,`fun-01`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why is power transmitted at high voltage?`,options:[`To reduce current and therefore resistive loss for the same delivered power`,`Because generators can only produce high voltage`,`To make it safer to touch`,`To reduce the frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Loss = I²R; lower current at the same power sharply reduces loss.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why does AC pair naturally with transformers, while DC does not?`,options:[`Transformers need a changing magnetic flux, which only alternating current provides continuously`,`Transformers only work below 100 V`,`DC is always more powerful`,`Transformers need high frequency only`],answer:0,explain:`A steady DC current gives a steady, non-inducing flux once established.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An inverter converts DC to AC by...`,options:[`actively and repeatedly reversing the polarity presented to the output using switching devices`,`passively filtering the DC`,`simply connecting the battery directly to the load`,`increasing the DC voltage only`],answer:0,explain:`This is the H-bridge's job.`},{id:`k4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Transmission loss through a 1 Ω line carrying 50 A (W)?`,answer:2500,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`P = I²R = 2500 × 1 = 2500 W.`}],summary:[`Power is transmitted at high voltage to minimise current and resistive loss for the same delivered power.`,`Transformers only work on AC, because they require a continuously changing magnetic flux.`,`This is the fundamental reason grids and most appliances use AC.`,`An inverter actively synthesises AC from a DC source by repeatedly reversing polarity — it does not simply "convert" it passively.`],homework:{id:`F3.M1.L1.hw`,title:`Transmission loss practice`,brief:`Apply P_loss = I²R at different voltages.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A 50 kW load through a 1.5 Ω line at 5000 V. Current (A)?`,answer:10,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`I = P/V = 50,000/5000 = 10 A.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Loss for that same current and line resistance (W)?`,answer:150,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`Loss = I²R = 100 × 1.5 = 150 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`A colleague suggests transmitting power as DC at the same voltage used at the consumer's premises, avoiding the "complication" of AC altogether. Explain the practical problem with this suggestion.`,model:`Transmitting at the low voltage used at the premises would require enormous currents for any significant power, causing very large resistive losses (P_loss = I²R) and requiring impractically thick conductors over transmission distances. AC allows efficient, lossless transformation to a high transmission voltage and back down, using transformers; a pure low-voltage DC scheme has no equally practical way to do this at scale.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M1.L2`,title:`Sine waves: amplitude, period, frequency, phase`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Define amplitude, period, frequency and angular frequency for a sine wave.`,`Convert between period and frequency, and between frequency and angular frequency.`,`Define phase and phase difference between two waveforms of the same frequency.`,`Write the standard equation for a sinusoidal voltage or current.`],introduction:`Mains and inverter AC voltages are, ideally, sine waves. This lesson gives you the vocabulary to describe one precisely: how big it is, how fast it repeats, and where it sits in time relative to another sine wave — all needed before reactance, power and transformers make sense.`,lecture:`### The sine wave equation

v(t) = V_pk · sin(ωt + φ)

- **V_pk** (amplitude/peak) — the maximum value the waveform reaches.
- **ω (angular frequency)**, in radians per second — how fast the angle ωt advances.
- **φ (phase)**, in radians or degrees — where the waveform sits at t = 0, relative to a reference.

### Period and frequency

The **period (T)** is the time for one complete cycle. The **frequency (f)** is the number of cycles per second (hertz, Hz): f = 1/T. **Angular frequency (ω)** relates to frequency by ω = 2πf, because one full cycle corresponds to 2π radians.

### Phase and phase difference

[[phase|Phase]] describes where in its cycle a waveform is at a given instant. Two sine waves of the SAME frequency can be shifted in time relative to each other — this shift, expressed as an angle, is their **phase difference**. A phase difference of 0° means the waveforms rise and fall together (in phase); 180° means one is the exact mirror of the other; 90° means one leads or lags the other by a quarter cycle. Phase difference between voltage and current in a circuit (caused by reactance, F3.M2) is central to AC power calculations (F3.M3).

### Why this matters for inverters

An inverter's output frequency (ideally a stable 50 or 60 Hz) and the phase relationship between its voltage and current (set by the load's reactance) both affect how well it matches grid requirements and how it should be rated and protected.

### Reading phase off a waveform plot

Given two plotted sine waves of the same frequency, the phase difference between them can be read directly from how far one waveform's zero-crossing (or peak) is shifted along the time axis relative to the other's, then expressed as a fraction of a full cycle and converted to degrees or radians — exactly the kind of reading the oscilloscope's cursors let you make directly on a captured trace.`,technical:`**Angular frequency as a rotating phasor.** A sine wave can be visualised as the vertical projection of a point rotating at constant angular speed ω around a circle of radius V_pk — this "phasor" picture (developed further in F3.M2) makes phase relationships between multiple waveforms far easier to reason about than working with the raw time-domain equations.

**Radians versus degrees.** Formulas use radians (ωt in radians), but engineers often quote phase in degrees for readability. 2π radians = 360°; converting between them is a simple scaling, but keep units consistent within one calculation.`,diagrams:[{id:`kirchhoff`,caption:`Two sine waves of the same frequency, one phase-shifted relative to the other, with amplitude, period and phase difference marked.`}],formulas:[{name:`Period–frequency`,expr:`f = 1/T,  T = 1/f`,where:[[`f`,`frequency`,`Hz`],[`T`,`period`,`s`]]},{name:`Angular frequency`,expr:`ω = 2πf`,where:[[`ω`,`angular frequency`,`rad/s`]]},{name:`Instantaneous sinusoidal voltage`,expr:`v(t) = V_pk · sin(ωt + φ)`,where:[[`V_pk`,`peak amplitude`,`V`],[`φ`,`phase angle`,`rad or °`]]}],examples:[{title:`Angular frequency of mains`,problem:`Find the angular frequency of a 50 Hz supply.`,steps:[`ω = 2πf`,`= 2π × 50`],answer:`ω ≈ 314.16 rad/s.`},{title:`Voltage at a given instant`,problem:`A 230 V rms, 50 Hz supply (take V_pk = 230√2 = 325.3 V) with zero phase. What is the instantaneous voltage at t = 2.5 ms?`,steps:[`ω = 2π × 50 = 314.16 rad/s`,`ωt = 314.16 × 0.0025 = 0.7854 rad`,`v(t) = 325.3 × sin(0.7854) = 325.3 × 0.7071`],answer:`≈ 230 V at that instant (this particular instant happens to land near the RMS-equivalent point of the cycle).`,check:`0.7854 rad = 45°, and sin(45°) = 0.7071 ≈ 1/√2, consistent with the result.`}],applications:`Multi-phase systems (used in larger inverters and three-phase grids, briefly referenced later in this programme) are built entirely from several sine waves of the same frequency held at fixed phase differences from each other — the same vocabulary this lesson introduces.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Confusing period and frequency.`,correction:`Period is time per cycle (seconds); frequency is cycles per second (Hz); they are reciprocals.`},{mistake:`Mixing degrees and radians within one calculation.`,correction:`Convert consistently: 2π rad = 360°.`},{mistake:`Assuming "phase difference" only applies to different frequencies.`,correction:`Phase difference is meaningful specifically between waveforms of the SAME frequency; different frequencies drift relative to each other and have no fixed phase relationship.`}],demo:{title:`See a real 50 Hz trace`,text:`Capture a pure-sine waveform on the oscilloscope and read its period directly off the grid.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the oscilloscope`,`scope`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.sim(`AC waveform experiment`,`sim-03`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Period of a 60 Hz waveform (ms)?`,answer:16.67,tol:.01,unit:`ms`,explain:`T = 1/60 = 16.67 ms.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Angular frequency of a 60 Hz waveform (rad/s)?`,answer:376.99,tol:.01,unit:`rad/s`,explain:`ω = 2π×60 = 376.99 rad/s.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A phase difference of 180° between two same-frequency waveforms means they are...`,options:[`exact mirror images of each other`,`identical`,`a quarter cycle apart`,`at different frequencies`],answer:0,explain:`180° is a half-cycle shift.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Phase difference is a meaningful concept between...`,options:[`two waveforms of the same frequency`,`any two waveforms regardless of frequency`,`only DC signals`,`only square waves`],answer:0,explain:`Different frequencies drift relative to each other.`}],summary:[`A sine wave is described by amplitude (peak), frequency (or period) and phase.`,`f = 1/T and ω = 2πf relate frequency, period and angular frequency.`,`Phase difference is meaningful between waveforms of the SAME frequency; it describes their relative timing.`,`v(t) = V_pk·sin(ωt + φ) is the standard sinusoidal voltage equation.`],homework:{id:`F3.M1.L2.hw`,title:`Sine wave parameters`,brief:`Compute period, frequency and angular frequency.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Frequency of a waveform with period 4 ms (Hz)?`,answer:250,tol:.01,unit:`Hz`,explain:`f = 1/0.004 = 250 Hz.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Angular frequency of a 400 Hz waveform (rad/s)?`,answer:2513.27,tol:.01,unit:`rad/s`,explain:`ω = 2π×400 ≈ 2513.27 rad/s.`}],written:[{prompt:`Two inverters are meant to feed the same load in parallel. Explain, using the idea of phase, why their output waveforms must be kept at a fixed, matched phase relationship (not just the same frequency) for this to work safely.`,model:`Matching frequency alone is not enough: if the two inverters' outputs are at the same frequency but a different (or drifting) phase, their instantaneous voltages will differ at most moments, driving a large circulating current between them even though both claim the "same" voltage and frequency. Only when frequency AND phase are matched do the two waveforms track each other instant by instant, so they can be safely paralleled onto the same load without a large phase-driven circulating current.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M1.L3`,title:`Peak, average and RMS`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Define peak, average and RMS values of an AC waveform.`,`Derive and apply V_rms = V_pk/√2 for a pure sine wave.`,`Explain why RMS, not peak or average, is used for AC power calculations.`,`Compute RMS for a simple non-sinusoidal (square) waveform.`],introduction:`This lesson picks up directly where the Virtual Instruments laboratory's oscilloscope module (F5.M3.L2) left RMS as "the power-equivalent value" and derives WHY that is true, and HOW to compute it for a sine wave and for other waveform shapes you will meet throughout this course and the Build Your Own Inverter laboratory.`,lecture:`### Three different "sizes" for one waveform

- **Peak (V_pk)**: maximum instantaneous value.
- **Average**: the mean value over one full cycle. For a symmetric AC waveform (equal positive and negative halves), the average over a FULL cycle is exactly zero — not useful on its own. (A "rectified average", the average of the absolute value, is sometimes used instead, e.g. by cheap AC meters, F5.M3.L2.)
- **RMS (root mean square)**: the value that delivers the SAME heating power into a resistor as the actual time-varying waveform does.

### Deriving RMS for a sine wave

Instantaneous power into a resistance R is p(t) = v(t)²/R. Averaging v(t)² over one cycle for v(t) = V_pk·sin(ωt) gives an average of V_pk²/2 (because sin² averages to 1/2 over a full cycle). The RMS value is defined as the square root of this mean square:

**V_rms = √(V_pk² / 2) = V_pk / √2 ≈ 0.707 · V_pk**

### RMS of other waveforms

RMS depends on SHAPE, not just peak. An ideal square wave spends its entire cycle at ±V_pk, so its mean square is simply V_pk² (not V_pk²/2), giving **V_rms = V_pk** for a square wave — no √2 reduction at all. This is exactly the distinction F5.M3.L2 relied on without deriving; here is the derivation.

### Why RMS, not peak, for power

Because P = V_rms²/R directly, using RMS values in power calculations gives the correct AVERAGE power delivered — exactly matching what a DC source of the same RMS voltage would deliver into the same resistance. Using peak or average (zero) values instead gives systematically wrong answers.`,technical:`**The "root mean square" name describes the calculation exactly.** Square the instantaneous value, find its MEAN over one cycle, then take the SQUARE ROOT — "root of the mean of the square", read backward to "root-mean-square". This calculation generalises to ANY periodic waveform, not just sine waves, which is why an oscilloscope or true-RMS meter can report a correct RMS value for a square wave, a modified sine, or an arbitrary distorted waveform, by actually computing this integral (or its sampled equivalent) rather than assuming a sine shape.

**Crest factor**, introduced in F5.M3.L2, is V_pk/V_rms: √2 ≈ 1.414 for a pure sine, exactly 1 for an ideal square wave, and higher for peaky waveforms.`,diagrams:[{id:`rc-curve`,caption:`v(t) and v(t)² for a sine wave over one cycle, showing why the mean of v² is V_pk²/2 and RMS follows from its square root.`}],formulas:[{name:`RMS of a pure sine`,expr:`V_rms = V_pk / √2 ≈ 0.707 · V_pk`,where:[[`V_pk`,`peak value`,`V`]]},{name:`RMS of an ideal square wave`,expr:`V_rms = V_pk`,where:[]},{name:`Power from RMS`,expr:`P = V_rms² / R = V_rms · I_rms (resistive load)`,where:[[`P`,`average power`,`W`]]}],examples:[{title:`RMS of UK/EU mains`,problem:`230 V mains is quoted as an RMS value. What is its peak voltage?`,steps:[`V_pk = V_rms × √2`,`= 230 × 1.4142`],answer:`≈ 325.3 V peak.`},{title:`Power into a resistive load`,problem:`A 230 V rms supply drives a 46 Ω resistive heater. Average power delivered?`,steps:[`P = V_rms² / R`,`= 230² / 46 = 52,900 / 46`],answer:`≈ 1150 W.`,check:`I_rms = 230/46 = 5 A; P = V·I = 230×5 = 1150 W. ✓ consistent.`},{title:`RMS of a 48 V square-wave inverter output`,problem:`A square-wave inverter output has a 48 V peak. What is its RMS value, and the average power it delivers into a 12 Ω resistive load?`,steps:[`V_rms = V_pk = 48 V (square wave, no √2 reduction)`,`P = V_rms²/R = 48²/12 = 2304/12`],answer:`V_rms = 48 V; P = 192 W.`}],applications:`Every power rating, current rating and protective-device sizing calculation from F3.M3 onward uses RMS values. Inverter and component datasheets state RMS voltage and current for exactly this reason.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Using peak voltage in a power calculation.`,correction:`Use RMS voltage; peak overstates average power by a factor related to the crest factor.`},{mistake:`Assuming V_rms = V_pk/√2 for every waveform shape.`,correction:`That relationship holds only for a pure sine. A square wave has V_rms = V_pk; other shapes fall in between or beyond.`},{mistake:`Treating the simple average (which is zero for symmetric AC) as a useful "size" of the waveform.`,correction:`Use RMS, or rectified average with the correct shape-specific correction factor, never the raw zero average.`}],demo:{title:`Confirm RMS with the oscilloscope`,text:`Capture a square wave and a pure sine of the same peak voltage on the scope and compare their RMS cursor readings against this lesson's formulas.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the oscilloscope`,`scope`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.sim(`RMS and power experiment`,`sim-04`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`RMS of a pure sine with 100 V peak (V)?`,answer:70.7,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`100/√2 = 70.7 V.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Peak of a 120 V rms pure sine (V)?`,answer:169.7,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`120×√2 = 169.7 V.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`RMS of an ideal square wave equals...`,options:[`its peak value`,`peak/√2`,`half its peak`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`A square wave is at full amplitude the whole cycle.`},{id:`k4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Average power into a 23 Ω resistor from a 230 V rms supply (W)?`,answer:2300,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`P = 230²/23 = 2300 W.`}],summary:[`Peak is the maximum instantaneous value; the simple average of a symmetric AC waveform is zero and not useful.`,`RMS is the value that delivers the same heating power into a resistor as the real waveform — "root of the mean of the square".`,`V_rms = V_pk/√2 only for a pure sine; V_rms = V_pk for an ideal square wave.`,`Power calculations (P = V_rms²/R) require RMS values, not peak or average.`],homework:{id:`F3.M1.L3.hw`,title:`RMS and power practice`,brief:`Compute RMS values and resulting power.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`RMS of a pure sine with 12 V peak (V)?`,answer:8.49,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`12/√2 = 8.49 V.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power into a 9 Ω resistor from a 120 V rms source (W)?`,answer:1600,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`P = 120²/9 = 1600 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`A modified-sine inverter has the same 325 V peak as a pure-sine inverter, but its RMS output is lower than the pure sine's 230 V. Explain, in terms of this lesson, why equal peaks do not mean equal RMS, and what this implies about the power each would deliver to the same resistive load.`,model:`RMS depends on the waveform shape, not just the peak. A modified sine holds zero for part of each half-cycle, so its mean-square value (and hence RMS) is lower than a pure sine of the same peak, which never drops to zero except at the zero crossings. Because average power into a resistive load is P = V_rms²/R, the modified-sine inverter delivers less average power to the same resistive load than the pure-sine inverter, even with identical peak voltages.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M1.L4`,title:`Non-sinusoidal waveforms`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Identify square, modified-sine, PWM and rectified waveforms by their time-domain shape.`,`Explain why an inverter's raw switched output is not a pure sine.`,`State which inverter waveform families require filtering to approach a pure sine, and why.`],introduction:`Real inverters rarely produce a textbook pure sine directly from their switches — filtering is what gets them there. This lesson surveys the waveform families you will build, measure and reason about throughout the rest of this programme: square, modified-sine, PWM and rectified.`,lecture:`### The waveform families

- **Square wave**: the switches present +V_dc then −V_dc, with an instant transition, at the fundamental frequency. Simplest to generate, but the roughest output (high THD, F5.M3.L3).
- **Modified sine (quasi-square)**: adds a zero-voltage dwell within each half-cycle, closer in RMS and harmonic content to a sine than a plain square wave, without needing an output filter — the classic "modified sine wave inverter" compromise.
- **PWM / SPWM (sinusoidal pulse-width modulation)**: the switches pulse on and off at a much higher carrier frequency, with the WIDTH of each pulse varying to trace out a sine-shaped average. Unfiltered, this looks nothing like a sine on an oscilloscope (F5.M3.L1) — it only becomes one after the output LC filter removes the high-frequency carrier content.
- **Rectified**: not an inverter output at all, but the result of converting AC back to DC (bridge rectifier, F3.M5) — included here because you will recognise its shape (all-positive "humps") when troubleshooting or analysing a rectifier stage.

### Why not just produce a pure sine directly?

A switching device is fundamentally a two-state (on/off) device — it is efficient precisely because it is either fully on (low loss) or fully off (no current), never in between dissipating power as a linear amplifier would. Producing a smoothly varying sine directly, rather than through fast switching plus filtering, would require linear (non-switching) amplification, which is far less efficient at the power levels inverters operate at. SPWM followed by filtering gets the efficiency of switching with an output close to a pure sine.`,technical:`**Why "sinusoidal" PWM specifically.** The pulse width in SPWM is set by comparing a sine-shaped reference signal against a high-frequency triangular carrier (F3.M5, Build lab project D) — this specific modulation scheme is chosen because its filtered output approximates a sine wave more closely, with lower distortion, than simpler fixed-pattern switching schemes.

**Multi-level inverters** (a more advanced topology, mentioned here for completeness, not required at this level) use more than two output voltage levels to approach a sine even more closely before filtering, at the cost of more switching devices and more complex control.`,diagrams:[{id:`quasi-square`,caption:`Square, modified-sine, unfiltered SPWM and filtered pure-sine waveforms compared over the same time axis.`}],formulas:[{name:`Crest factor`,expr:`CF = V_pk / V_rms`,where:[[`CF`,`crest factor`,`dimensionless`]],note:`CF = √2 ≈ 1.414 for a pure sine; CF = 1 for an ideal square wave — one quick way to recognise a waveform family from a meter or scope reading.`}],examples:[{title:`Why switching is efficient but produces a rough raw waveform`,problem:`Explain why a switch dissipates little power while fully on or fully off, but a linear (non-switching) voltage regulator dissipates significant power when regulating the same voltage down.`,steps:[`A fully-on switch has very low resistance, so P = I²R is small despite high current`,`A fully-off switch has (ideally) zero current, so P = 0 regardless of voltage across it`,`A linear regulator instead drops the "extra" voltage across itself while current still flows, dissipating P = I × V_dropped continuously`],answer:`Switching devices avoid the continuous V×I loss a linear regulator suffers, which is why switching conversion dominates at the power levels inverters operate at, at the cost of a raw output that needs shaping (filtering) rather than being inherently smooth.`},{title:`Recognising a waveform from its trace`,problem:`An oscilloscope trace shows three distinct levels per half-cycle: +Vdc held for part of the cycle, 0 V for the rest, mirrored in the negative half. Which waveform family is this?`,steps:[`Not a plain square wave (which has only two levels, no zero dwell)`,`Not filtered SPWM/pure sine (which would show a smooth curve)`,`Three levels with a zero dwell matches the modified-sine definition`],answer:`Modified sine (quasi-square).`}],applications:`This exact survey maps directly onto the Build Your Own Inverter laboratory's projects B (square), C (modified sine) and D (pure sine, filtered SPWM) — and onto what you will actually see on the oscilloscope in each.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`"An inverter's switches produce a sine wave directly."`,correction:`Switches produce square, modified-sine or PWM waveforms; a pure sine requires filtering the switched output.`},{mistake:`Confusing rectified DC with an inverter output waveform.`,correction:`Rectification converts AC to DC (the opposite direction); it produces an all-positive "humped" shape, not an inverter output.`},{mistake:`Assuming modified sine needs an output filter like SPWM does.`,correction:`Modified sine is produced directly by switch timing, without a following filter; SPWM specifically needs filtering to approach a sine.`}],demo:{title:`Compare all four shapes on the scope`,text:`Step through square, modified-sine, PWM and rectified on this lab's oscilloscope and match each to the description above.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the oscilloscope`,`scope`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.build(`Project B: square-wave inverter`,`bld-b`),e.build(`Project D: pure sine-wave inverter`,`bld-d`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which waveform has a deliberate zero-voltage dwell within each half-cycle?`,options:[`Modified sine`,`Plain square wave`,`Pure sine`,`Rectified DC`],answer:0,explain:`The zero dwell distinguishes modified sine from a plain square wave.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Unfiltered SPWM, viewed on an oscilloscope at a fine timebase, looks like...`,options:[`a fast train of varying-width pulses, not a smooth sine`,`a smooth sine directly`,`a plain square wave`,`a DC level`],answer:0,explain:`Filtering is required afterward to produce a smooth sine.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Rectification converts...`,options:[`AC to DC`,`DC to AC`,`frequency to voltage`,`voltage to current`],answer:0,explain:`Rectification is the opposite direction from inversion.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why do switching devices (rather than linear amplifiers) dominate inverter design?`,options:[`They dissipate little power while fully on or fully off, unlike a continuously-dropping linear stage`,`They are always cheaper`,`They inherently produce a pure sine`,`They require no control circuitry`],answer:0,explain:`Switching avoids the continuous V×I loss of linear regulation.`}],summary:[`Square, modified-sine, PWM and rectified are the main waveform families you will encounter and build throughout this programme.`,`A switching device is efficient because it is either fully on or fully off, never continuously dropping voltage like a linear regulator.`,`Square and modified-sine waveforms are produced directly by switch timing; SPWM requires a following filter to approach a pure sine.`,`Rectification (AC to DC) produces a recognisable all-positive "humped" shape, the opposite direction from inversion.`],homework:{id:`F3.M1.L4.hw`,title:`Waveform identification`,brief:`Identify waveform families from their description.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A waveform that switches instantly between +Vdc and −Vdc with no intermediate level is...`,options:[`a plain square wave`,`modified sine`,`filtered pure sine`,`rectified DC`],answer:0,explain:`Two levels, no zero dwell: a plain square wave.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain why a pure-sine inverter is more complex (and usually more expensive) to build than a square-wave inverter, in terms of the waveform families covered in this lesson.`,model:`A square-wave inverter only needs switches operated at the fundamental frequency with no following filter. A pure-sine inverter needs SPWM switching at a much higher carrier frequency, precise pulse-width control to trace out a sine-shaped average, and an output LC filter sized to remove the carrier-frequency content while passing the fundamental — all of which add components, control complexity and cost compared with simple square-wave switching.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why is power transmitted at high voltage?`,options:[`To reduce current and resistive loss for the same power`,`Because generators can only make high voltage`,`To make it safer to touch`,`To reduce frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Loss = I²R.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Period of a 50 Hz waveform (ms)?`,answer:20,tol:.01,unit:`ms`,explain:`T = 1/50 = 20 ms.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A phase difference is meaningful between...`,options:[`two waveforms of the same frequency`,`any two waveforms`,`only DC signals`,`only harmonics`],answer:0,explain:`Different frequencies have no fixed phase relationship.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`RMS of a pure sine with 50 V peak (V)?`,answer:35.4,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`50/√2 = 35.4 V.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`RMS of an ideal square wave equals...`,options:[`its peak value`,`peak/√2`,`half its peak`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`Full amplitude for the whole cycle.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`SPWM requires a following output filter mainly because...`,options:[`its unfiltered switched output carries high-frequency carrier content, not a smooth sine`,`it has the wrong fundamental frequency`,`it is DC, not AC`,`filters increase switching frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Filtering removes the carrier-frequency content, leaving a sine-like fundamental.`}]},practicals:[]},{id:`F3.M2`,title:`Reactance and impedance`,summary:`How capacitors and inductors oppose AC current in a frequency-dependent way, how reactance and resistance combine as impedance, and how resonance and filters follow from these ideas.`,lessons:[{id:`F3.M2.L1`,title:`Capacitive reactance`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Define capacitive reactance and compute it at a given frequency.`,`Explain why a capacitor passes AC more easily at higher frequency and blocks DC.`,`Explain the 90° phase relationship between voltage and current in an ideal capacitor.`,`Relate capacitive reactance to the LCR meter readings from F5.M4.L2.`],introduction:`A capacitor's opposition to current is not a fixed resistance — it depends on frequency. This single idea, [[reactance|capacitive reactance]], explains why capacitors block DC entirely yet pass high-frequency signals almost freely, and is the same physics behind the LCR meter's impedance reading you met in F5.M4.L2.`,lecture:`### What reactance is

[[reactance|Reactance]] (symbol X) is the AC opposition to current from a capacitor or inductor, measured in ohms like resistance, but — unlike resistance — it depends on frequency and causes a 90° phase shift between voltage and current rather than dissipating energy as heat.

### Capacitive reactance

**X_C = 1 / (2πfC)**

As frequency f rises, X_C falls: a capacitor opposes current LESS at higher frequency. As f approaches zero (DC), X_C approaches infinity: an ideal capacitor blocks DC completely once fully charged, which is exactly why a capacitor used as a DC-bus reservoir charges up and then stops drawing further current once the ripple it is smoothing has been absorbed.

### Why a capacitor behaves this way

A capacitor stores energy in its electric field by accumulating charge on its plates: i = C(dv/dt), current is proportional to how FAST the voltage is changing, not to the voltage itself. A rapidly changing (high-frequency) voltage drives a large current; a slowly changing (low-frequency) voltage drives little current; a constant (DC) voltage drives none once charged.

### The 90° phase relationship

For an ideal capacitor, current LEADS voltage by 90°: the current is largest exactly when the voltage is changing fastest (crossing zero), and smallest when the voltage is at its peak (momentarily flat, not changing). This phase relationship — current leading voltage in a capacitor — is central to understanding power factor (F3.M3) and resonance (F3.M2.L4).`,technical:`**Impedance as a complex number.** A full treatment represents capacitive reactance as the imaginary quantity −j/(ωC) (using complex/phasor notation), which automatically encodes both the magnitude (1/ωC) and the 90° phase shift in one expression. This programme uses the magnitude form (X_C = 1/2πfC) and states the phase relationship separately, which is sufficient for the measurement and power-factor work this course requires.

**Why LCR meters must specify a test frequency (recall F5.M4.L2).** Because X_C depends on frequency, an LCR meter's capacitance READING is largely frequency-independent for a healthy capacitor (it is measuring C itself), but its IMPEDANCE (Z) reading changes with the chosen test frequency — this is exactly the Z-versus-frequency behaviour you measured on this lab's LCR meter.`,diagrams:[{id:`rc-curve`,caption:`Capacitive reactance falling as frequency rises, following X_C = 1/2πfC, with the DC (f→0) limit shown as X_C→∞.`}],formulas:[{name:`Capacitive reactance`,expr:`X_C = 1 / (2πfC)`,where:[[`X_C`,`capacitive reactance`,`Ω`],[`f`,`frequency`,`Hz`],[`C`,`capacitance`,`F`]]}],examples:[{title:`Reactance of a filter capacitor at mains frequency`,problem:`Find the reactance of a 100 µF capacitor at 50 Hz.`,steps:[`X_C = 1 / (2π × 50 × 100×10⁻⁶)`,`= 1 / (2π × 0.005) = 1 / 0.0314`],answer:`≈ 31.83 Ω.`},{title:`Same capacitor at a much higher frequency`,problem:`Find the reactance of the same 100 µF capacitor at 20 kHz (a typical switching frequency), and compare it with the 50 Hz value.`,steps:[`X_C = 1 / (2π × 20,000 × 100×10⁻⁶)`,`= 1 / (2π × 2) = 1 / 12.566`],answer:`≈ 0.0796 Ω — about 400 times smaller than at 50 Hz, confirming the capacitor is a much better path for high-frequency content than for mains-frequency current.`,check:`20,000/50 = 400, and reactance is inversely proportional to frequency, so a 400× frequency increase gives a 400× reactance decrease. ✓`}],applications:`This is exactly why a bypass/decoupling capacitor placed across a DC bus is effective at absorbing high-frequency switching ripple while barely affecting the low-frequency (or DC) bus voltage itself — low reactance at high frequency, very high reactance near DC.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating capacitive reactance as a fixed resistance value.`,correction:`X_C depends on frequency; always state the frequency a reactance value applies to.`},{mistake:`"A capacitor passes DC once it is fully charged."`,correction:`An ideal, fully-charged capacitor passes NO further DC current; it blocks DC completely in steady state.`},{mistake:`Assuming reactance dissipates power the way resistance does.`,correction:`Ideal reactance stores and returns energy each cycle; it does not dissipate heat (real capacitors have some loss, modelled as ESR, F5.M4.L2).`}],demo:{title:`Measure impedance versus frequency`,text:`Sweep the LCR meter's test frequency on a capacitor case and watch its impedance change exactly as X_C = 1/2πfC predicts.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the LCR meter`,`lcr`)},activities:[e.instrument(`LCR meter`,`lcr`),e.lib(`Capacitors: electrolytic and film`,`capacitor`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`X_C of a 47 µF capacitor at 50 Hz (Ω)?`,answer:67.7,tol:.02,unit:`Ω`,explain:`X_C = 1/(2π×50×47e-6) ≈ 67.7 Ω.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`As frequency rises, capacitive reactance...`,options:[`falls`,`rises`,`stays constant`,`becomes resistive`],answer:0,explain:`X_C is inversely proportional to frequency.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In an ideal capacitor, current...`,options:[`leads voltage by 90°`,`lags voltage by 90°`,`is in phase with voltage`,`is always zero`],answer:0,explain:`Current is largest when voltage is changing fastest, which occurs before the voltage peak.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An ideal capacitor in steady state, with a constant DC voltage applied, carries...`,options:[`no further current`,`a constant current equal to C×V`,`an increasing current`,`a current equal to V/R`],answer:0,explain:`A constant voltage has dv/dt = 0, so i = C(dv/dt) = 0.`}],summary:[`Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(2πfC) falls as frequency rises and approaches infinity as frequency approaches zero (DC).`,`A capacitor's current depends on how fast its voltage changes (i = C·dv/dt), not on the voltage itself.`,`Current leads voltage by 90° in an ideal capacitor.`,`This frequency dependence is why capacitors pass high-frequency switching ripple while blocking DC — and why LCR meter impedance readings change with test frequency.`],homework:{id:`F3.M2.L1.hw`,title:`Capacitive reactance practice`,brief:`Compute X_C at different frequencies.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`X_C of a 220 µF capacitor at 50 Hz (Ω)?`,answer:14.47,tol:.02,unit:`Ω`,explain:`X_C = 1/(2π×50×220e-6) ≈ 14.47 Ω.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`X_C of the same capacitor at 1 kHz (Ω)?`,answer:.72,tol:.03,unit:`Ω`,explain:`X_C = 1/(2π×1000×220e-6) ≈ 0.72 Ω.`}],written:[{prompt:`A reservoir capacitor on a DC bus is observed to pass essentially no current at steady state, yet clearly carries significant ripple current when examined on an oscilloscope. Explain both observations using capacitive reactance.`,model:`At steady DC, the capacitor's voltage is constant, so dv/dt ≈ 0 and essentially no current flows through it — equivalently, its reactance at zero frequency is effectively infinite. The ripple is a higher-frequency component superimposed on the DC level; at that higher frequency, the capacitor's reactance is much lower, so it readily carries the ripple current, absorbing it and smoothing the bus voltage.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M2.L2`,title:`Inductive reactance`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Define inductive reactance and compute it at a given frequency.`,`Explain why an inductor opposes rapid current changes and passes DC freely.`,`Explain the 90° phase relationship between voltage and current in an ideal inductor.`,`Contrast inductive and capacitive reactance's frequency dependence.`],introduction:`An inductor is capacitive reactance's mirror image: where a capacitor opposes LOW-frequency current, an inductor opposes HIGH-frequency current. This lesson derives inductive reactance and its opposite-direction frequency dependence, setting up resonance (F3.M2.L4) where the two effects cancel.`,lecture:`### Inductive reactance

**X_L = 2πfL**

As frequency f rises, X_L RISES: an inductor opposes current MORE at higher frequency. As f approaches zero (DC), X_L approaches zero: an ideal inductor offers no opposition to steady DC current at all (only its winding's own DC resistance limits current, F5.M4.L2's DCR check).

### Why an inductor behaves this way

An inductor opposes CHANGE in current by generating a back-EMF proportional to the rate of change: v = L(di/dt). A rapidly changing (high-frequency) current induces a large opposing voltage; a slowly changing (low-frequency) current induces little; a constant (DC) current induces none, once established.

### The 90° phase relationship

For an ideal inductor, current LAGS voltage by 90° — the mirror image of the capacitor's relationship. Voltage is largest exactly when current is changing fastest; current is largest when voltage is momentarily zero (at a point where di/dt is largest).

### Capacitor versus inductor: opposite frequency behaviour

| | Low frequency / DC | High frequency |
|---|---|---|
| Capacitor | Blocks (high X_C) | Passes easily (low X_C) |
| Inductor | Passes easily (low X_L) | Blocks (high X_L) |

This opposite behaviour is exactly what makes LC filters work (F3.M2.L4): a capacitor and inductor arranged together can be designed to pass one frequency range while blocking another.

### Why this matters for inverter output stages

An output choke (series inductor) placed after an SPWM bridge presents low reactance to the 50/60 Hz fundamental, letting it pass through largely unimpeded, while presenting high reactance to the much higher switching-frequency content, blocking a significant part of it from reaching the load directly. This single component's frequency-dependent behaviour is doing real, calculable work in shaping the inverter's output waveform, well before the matching filter capacitor (F3.M2.L1) is even considered.`,technical:`**Why an inductor's opposition is called "inductive reactance" and not resistance.** Like capacitive reactance, X_L does not dissipate energy in an ideal inductor — it stores energy in a magnetic field and returns it each cycle. Real inductors have winding resistance (DCR) in series, which DOES dissipate energy as heat — this is why F5.M4.L2's inductor test checks DC resistance separately from inductance.

**Inductive reactance in impedance (complex) form.** A full treatment represents inductive reactance as +jωL, the opposite sign convention from capacitive reactance's −j/(ωC) — this sign difference is precisely why capacitive and inductive reactances can cancel each other at a particular frequency (resonance).`,diagrams:[{id:`rl-curve`,caption:`Inductive reactance rising with frequency (X_L = 2πfL), the mirror image of capacitive reactance's falling curve.`}],formulas:[{name:`Inductive reactance`,expr:`X_L = 2πfL`,where:[[`X_L`,`inductive reactance`,`Ω`],[`L`,`inductance`,`H`]]}],examples:[{title:`Reactance of an output choke at mains frequency`,problem:`Find the reactance of a 1.2 mH inductor at 50 Hz.`,steps:[`X_L = 2π × 50 × 1.2×10⁻³`,`= 2π × 0.06`],answer:`≈ 0.377 Ω.`},{title:`Same inductor at a switching frequency`,problem:`Find the reactance of the same 1.2 mH inductor at 20 kHz, and compare with the 50 Hz value.`,steps:[`X_L = 2π × 20,000 × 1.2×10⁻³`,`= 2π × 24`],answer:`≈ 150.8 Ω — about 400 times larger than at 50 Hz, the same 400× factor as frequency, confirming the inductor is a much better block for high-frequency content than for mains-frequency current.`,check:`Reactance is directly proportional to frequency for an inductor, so the ratio of reactances equals the ratio of frequencies: 20,000/50 = 400. ✓`}],applications:`This is exactly why the output inductor in an LC filter (F3.M2.L4, Build lab project D) passes the 50/60 Hz fundamental with little opposition while strongly blocking the high-frequency switching content — the direct complement to the filter capacitor's own frequency-dependent behaviour.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`"An inductor blocks DC the way a capacitor does."`,correction:`The opposite: an ideal inductor offers NO opposition to steady DC current; it is high-frequency AC that an inductor opposes.`},{mistake:`Confusing the frequency dependence direction of X_L and X_C.`,correction:`X_C falls with frequency; X_L rises with frequency — memorise the table, not just one formula.`},{mistake:`Ignoring winding resistance (DCR) when reasoning about a real inductor's behaviour at DC.`,correction:`A real inductor still has some resistance at DC (its DCR), even though its ideal reactance is zero there.`}],demo:{title:`Measure an inductor's impedance versus frequency`,text:`Sweep the LCR meter's test frequency on an inductor case and watch its impedance rise exactly as X_L = 2πfL predicts — the opposite trend from the capacitor.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the LCR meter`,`lcr`)},activities:[e.instrument(`LCR meter`,`lcr`),e.lib(`Power chokes and filter inductors`,`inductor`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`X_L of a 10 mH inductor at 50 Hz (Ω)?`,answer:3.14,tol:.02,unit:`Ω`,explain:`X_L = 2π×50×0.01 ≈ 3.14 Ω.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`As frequency rises, inductive reactance...`,options:[`rises`,`falls`,`stays constant`,`becomes resistive`],answer:0,explain:`X_L is directly proportional to frequency.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In an ideal inductor, current...`,options:[`lags voltage by 90°`,`leads voltage by 90°`,`is in phase with voltage`,`is always zero`],answer:0,explain:`The mirror image of the capacitor's relationship.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An ideal inductor at steady DC offers...`,options:[`no opposition at all (only winding resistance limits current)`,`infinite opposition`,`the same opposition as at high frequency`,`opposition equal to its DC resistance only at high frequency`],answer:0,explain:`X_L = 2πfL → 0 as f → 0.`}],summary:[`Inductive reactance X_L = 2πfL rises as frequency rises and falls to zero at DC.`,`An inductor opposes current CHANGE (v = L·di/dt); it offers no opposition to steady DC current.`,`Current lags voltage by 90° in an ideal inductor — the mirror image of the capacitor.`,`Capacitors and inductors have opposite frequency behaviour, which is exactly what makes LC filters work.`],homework:{id:`F3.M2.L2.hw`,title:`Inductive reactance practice`,brief:`Compute X_L at different frequencies.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`X_L of a 2 mH inductor at 50 Hz (Ω)?`,answer:.628,tol:.02,unit:`Ω`,explain:`X_L = 2π×50×0.002 ≈ 0.628 Ω.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`X_L of the same inductor at 10 kHz (Ω)?`,answer:125.7,tol:.02,unit:`Ω`,explain:`X_L = 2π×10,000×0.002 ≈ 125.7 Ω.`}],written:[{prompt:`An output inductor and an output capacitor are placed together after an SPWM bridge, forming an LC filter. Explain, using the opposite frequency behaviour of X_L and X_C, why this combination is more effective at passing the fundamental and blocking the switching frequency than either component alone.`,model:`The inductor has low reactance at the 50/60 Hz fundamental (passing it with little opposition in series) but high reactance at the switching frequency (blocking it in series). The capacitor has the opposite behaviour: high reactance at the fundamental (so it does not shunt it away) but low reactance at the switching frequency (shunting the high-frequency content to the return path). Together, in an LC arrangement, their complementary behaviour reinforces the filtering in both directions far more effectively than using just one component alone.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M2.L3`,title:`Impedance and phasors`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Combine resistance and reactance into impedance magnitude using Z = √(R² + X²).`,`Use a phasor diagram to represent the phase relationship between resistive and reactive components.`,`Compute impedance magnitude and phase angle for a simple R-X combination.`,`Relate impedance to the LCR meter's Z-mode readings from F5.M4.L2.`],introduction:`A real component is rarely a pure resistance, a pure capacitance or a pure inductance — it is usually some combination. [[impedance|Impedance]] (Z) combines resistance and reactance into one quantity describing the total AC opposition, exactly as the LCR meter's Z mode (F5.M4.L2) already computes for you.`,lecture:`### Combining resistance and reactance

Resistance (R) and reactance (X) cannot simply be added arithmetically, because they are 90° out of phase with each other (resistance dissipates energy in phase with current; reactance stores and returns it 90° out of phase). They combine the way the two legs of a right-angled triangle combine into its hypotenuse:

**Z = √(R² + X²)**

where X is the NET reactance — for a circuit with both capacitance and inductance, X = X_L − X_C (they partially or fully cancel, because they have opposite sign/phase effects).

### Phasor diagrams

A **phasor diagram** represents each quantity (voltage, current, or here resistance/reactance) as an arrow (vector) at the correct angle, rather than writing out full sine-wave equations. Resistance is drawn along one axis; reactance is drawn at 90° to it. The resultant impedance is the vector sum — literally the hypotenuse of a right triangle when there is only one reactance type, or the net reactance after X_L and X_C partially cancel.

### Phase angle of the impedance

The phase angle φ between the circuit's overall voltage and current is:

**φ = arctan(X / R)**

A purely resistive circuit has φ = 0° (voltage and current in phase). A purely reactive circuit approaches φ = ±90°. Real circuits with both resistance and reactance sit somewhere in between — this angle is exactly what determines the power factor covered in F3.M3.

### Relating this to the LCR meter

When F5.M4.L2's LCR meter reports Z at a given test frequency, it is computing precisely this: Z = √(X² + R²), using the component's own series resistance (ESR or DCR) as R and its calculated reactance at that frequency as X.`,technical:`**The full complex-number treatment.** Impedance is properly written as a complex number Z = R + jX, where j is the imaginary unit. The magnitude |Z| = √(R² + X²) is what this lesson uses; the phase angle arg(Z) = arctan(X/R) is the second piece of information the complex form carries. This programme works with magnitude and phase angle separately rather than full complex-number arithmetic, which is sufficient for the measurement and power-factor applications this course requires.

**Series versus parallel combination.** This lesson covers series R-X combinations, where impedances simply add (as complex numbers, hence the Pythagorean-style magnitude formula). Parallel combinations of reactive and resistive elements combine differently (by reciprocals, as for parallel resistors) — a more advanced topic flagged here but not required at this level.`,diagrams:[{id:`kirchhoff`,caption:`A phasor diagram showing resistance along one axis and net reactance at 90° to it, with impedance as the resultant vector and φ as its angle.`}],formulas:[{name:`Impedance magnitude`,expr:`Z = √(R² + X²)`,where:[[`Z`,`impedance magnitude`,`Ω`],[`R`,`resistance`,`Ω`],[`X`,`net reactance (X_L − X_C)`,`Ω`]]},{name:`Impedance phase angle`,expr:`φ = arctan(X / R)`,where:[[`φ`,`phase angle between voltage and current`,`° or rad`]]}],examples:[{title:`Impedance of a resistor and capacitor in series`,problem:`A 100 Ω resistor is in series with a capacitor whose reactance at the operating frequency is 75 Ω. Find the impedance magnitude and phase angle.`,steps:[`Z = √(100² + 75²) = √(10,000 + 5625) = √15,625`,`φ = arctan(75/100) = arctan(0.75)`],answer:`Z = 125 Ω; φ ≈ 36.9° (current leading voltage, since the reactance is capacitive).`,check:`100-75-125 is a scaled 4-3-5 right triangle, confirming Z = 125 Ω exactly.`},{title:`Impedance from an LCR meter Z-mode reading`,problem:`An LCR meter reports Z = 3.52 Ω for a 452 µF capacitor at 100 Hz, with a known ESR of 0.04 Ω. Verify this is consistent with X_C = 1/(2πfC) and Z = √(R²+X²).`,steps:[`X_C = 1/(2π×100×452×10⁻⁶) ≈ 3.52 Ω`,`Z = √(3.52² + 0.04²) = √(12.39 + 0.0016) ≈ 3.52 Ω (the tiny ESR barely changes the result)`],answer:`Consistent: the reported Z essentially equals X_C here, because ESR (0.04 Ω) is negligible compared with the reactance (3.52 Ω) at this frequency.`}],applications:`This is the exact calculation behind every "Z (impedance)" reading the LCR meter instrument in this lab produces for a capacitor or inductor case, at whatever test frequency you choose.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Adding resistance and reactance arithmetically (R + X).`,correction:`They are 90° out of phase; combine them as Z = √(R² + X²), not a simple sum.`},{mistake:`Forgetting that X_L and X_C have opposite sign and can cancel.`,correction:`Net reactance X = X_L − X_C; when both are present, they partially or fully offset each other (resonance, F3.M2.L4).`},{mistake:`Assuming impedance is purely a resistance concept.`,correction:`Impedance generalises resistance to include frequency-dependent, phase-shifting reactance; at DC (or for a purely resistive circuit), Z reduces to R.`}],demo:{title:`See Z computed from X and R`,text:`The LCR meter's Z mode on this lab's cases computes exactly Z = √(X² + R²) at your chosen test frequency.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the LCR meter`,`lcr`)},activities:[e.instrument(`LCR meter`,`lcr`),e.sim(`Impedance experiment`,`sim-04`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Z for R = 30 Ω and X = 40 Ω in series (Ω)?`,answer:50,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`√(900+1600) = √2500 = 50 Ω (3-4-5 triangle).`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A purely resistive circuit has a phase angle φ of...`,options:[`0°`,`90°`,`45°`,`180°`],answer:0,explain:`Voltage and current are in phase.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Net reactance in a circuit with both inductance and capacitance is...`,options:[`X_L − X_C`,`X_L + X_C`,`always zero`,`the larger of the two only`],answer:0,explain:`The two reactances have opposite sign/phase and partially cancel.`},{id:`k4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`φ for R = 10 Ω, X = 10 Ω (°, to 1 decimal)?`,answer:45,tol:.02,unit:`°`,explain:`arctan(10/10) = arctan(1) = 45°.`}],summary:[`Impedance combines resistance and reactance: Z = √(R² + X²), because they are 90° out of phase.`,`A phasor diagram represents resistance and reactance as perpendicular vectors, with impedance as the resultant.`,`Net reactance X = X_L − X_C when both inductance and capacitance are present; they partially cancel.`,`The phase angle φ = arctan(X/R) between voltage and current is exactly what determines power factor (F3.M3).`],homework:{id:`F3.M2.L3.hw`,title:`Impedance calculations`,brief:`Combine R and X into Z and φ.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Z for R = 6 Ω and X = 8 Ω (Ω)?`,answer:10,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`√(36+64)=√100=10 Ω (3-4-5 scaled).`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`φ for R = 6 Ω, X = 8 Ω (°, to 1 decimal)?`,answer:53.1,tol:.02,unit:`°`,explain:`arctan(8/6) ≈ 53.1°.`}],written:[{prompt:`A circuit has a 20 Ω resistor, a reactor with X_L = 50 Ω and a capacitor with X_C = 50 Ω, all in series. Find the net reactance and the overall impedance, and explain what has physically happened.`,model:`Net reactance X = X_L − X_C = 50 − 50 = 0 Ω. With no net reactance, the impedance reduces to Z = √(R² + 0²) = R = 20 Ω — the circuit behaves as if it were purely resistive at this frequency, because the inductive and capacitive reactances exactly cancel. This is the condition of resonance, covered in the next lesson.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M2.L4`,title:`Resonance and filters`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain what happens at a circuit's resonant frequency.`,`Compute the resonant frequency of a series LC combination.`,`Explain qualitatively how an LC low-pass filter attenuates frequencies above its cutoff.`,`Relate resonance and filtering to the inverter output filter and THD (F5.M3.L3).`],introduction:`At one specific frequency, an inductor's and a capacitor's reactances exactly cancel — this is [[resonance|resonance]], and it is the underlying reason an LC filter can be DESIGNED to pass one frequency range and block another, exactly the filter behind a pure-sine inverter's clean output.`,lecture:`### The resonant frequency

For a series L and C, net reactance X = X_L − X_C is zero when X_L = X_C:

2πf₀L = 1/(2πf₀C) → **f₀ = 1 / (2π√(LC))**

At this frequency f₀, the two reactances cancel exactly, leaving only resistance to oppose current — in a series circuit with very low resistance, this can allow a surprisingly LARGE current to flow for a given applied voltage (resonant rise), which matters both as a design opportunity and a risk (unwanted resonance in a real circuit can cause unexpectedly large voltages or currents).

### LC filters: deliberately shaping frequency response

An inverter's output LC filter is a deliberate application of L and C's opposite frequency behaviour (F3.M2.L1–L2), designed so that:

- Below the filter's cutoff frequency (set well above the fundamental, well below the switching/carrier frequency): the inductor's low reactance and the capacitor's high reactance let the fundamental pass through largely unaffected.
- Above the cutoff: the inductor's high reactance and the capacitor's low reactance combine to strongly attenuate the switching-frequency content.

This is precisely the mechanism behind F5.M3.L3's statement that "an output LC filter reduces THD by attenuating frequencies above the fundamental" — now derived from first principles rather than simply stated.

### Choosing where to place the cutoff

A filter's cutoff frequency is chosen well above the fundamental (so the fundamental is barely attenuated) and well below the switching frequency (so the switching content is strongly attenuated) — typically by at least a factor of 10 in each direction where practical, giving the filter margin to work effectively without needing impractically large components.`,technical:`**Resonance as a risk, not only a tool.** An LC combination's resonant frequency can sometimes coincide, unintentionally, with a harmonic of the switching frequency or another signal already present in a circuit, causing unexpectedly large voltages or currents at that frequency ("ringing"). Filter design deliberately chooses component values so that no unwanted resonance falls near a frequency the circuit actually produces.

**Quality factor (Q).** A resonant circuit's "sharpness" (how narrow the frequency range of large response is) is described by its quality factor, set by the ratio of reactance to resistance at resonance — a higher Q circuit is more selective but also raises the resonant current/voltage more sharply. This programme does not require Q calculations, but recognising that resonant behaviour can be sharper or gentler depending on resistance is useful context.`,diagrams:[{id:`lc-filter`,caption:`An LC low-pass filter's frequency response: the fundamental passes with little attenuation, while the switching-frequency content is strongly attenuated above the cutoff.`}],formulas:[{name:`Series resonant frequency`,expr:`f₀ = 1 / (2π√(LC))`,where:[[`f₀`,`resonant frequency`,`Hz`],[`L`,`inductance`,`H`],[`C`,`capacitance`,`F`]]}],examples:[{title:`Resonant frequency of an output filter`,problem:`An inverter's output filter uses a 1.2 mH inductor and a 10 µF capacitor. Find its resonant (cutoff) frequency.`,steps:[`f₀ = 1 / (2π√(LC)) = 1 / (2π√(1.2×10⁻³ × 10×10⁻⁶))`,`= 1 / (2π√(1.2×10⁻⁸)) = 1 / (2π × 1.095×10⁻⁴)`],answer:`≈ 1453 Hz.`,check:`This sits comfortably above a 50 Hz fundamental (by about 29×) and well below a 20 kHz switching frequency (by about 14×), a sensible filter placement.`},{title:`Checking filter placement against fundamental and switching frequency`,problem:`For the filter in the previous example (f₀ ≈ 1453 Hz), is it well placed between a 50 Hz fundamental and a 20 kHz switching frequency?`,steps:[`Ratio to fundamental: 1453/50 ≈ 29×, comfortably above 10×`,`Ratio to switching frequency: 20,000/1453 ≈ 13.8×, comfortably above 10×`],answer:`Yes — the cutoff sits well clear of both the fundamental and the switching frequency by more than a factor of 10 each way, giving the filter margin to work effectively.`}],applications:`This is the exact calculation behind Project D (pure sine-wave inverter) in the Build Your Own Inverter laboratory: choosing L and C values that place the filter's cutoff well above the fundamental and well below the switching frequency.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Placing a filter's cutoff too close to the fundamental frequency.`,correction:`This attenuates the fundamental itself, reducing output voltage and distorting the waveform; keep the cutoff well above the fundamental.`},{mistake:`Placing a filter's cutoff too close to the switching frequency.`,correction:`This fails to adequately attenuate switching content, leaving significant high-frequency ripple on the output.`},{mistake:`Assuming resonance is always a problem to avoid.`,correction:`Resonance is deliberately exploited in filter design (and other applications); it only becomes a problem when it occurs unintentionally at an unwanted frequency.`}],demo:{title:`Compare filtered and unfiltered output`,text:`View unfiltered SPWM and filtered pure-sine output side by side on the oscilloscope to see the LC filter's effect directly.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the oscilloscope`,`scope`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.build(`Project D: pure sine-wave inverter (SPWM + LC filter)`,`bld-d`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`At the resonant frequency of a series LC circuit, net reactance is...`,options:[`zero (X_L and X_C cancel)`,`at its maximum`,`always infinite`,`equal to resistance always`],answer:0,explain:`X_L = X_C at resonance, so X_L − X_C = 0.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Resonant frequency of a 2 mH, 50 µF LC combination (Hz, to the nearest whole number)?`,answer:503,tol:.03,unit:`Hz`,explain:`f₀ = 1/(2π√(2e-3×50e-6)) ≈ 503 Hz.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An inverter output LC filter's cutoff should sit...`,options:[`well above the fundamental and well below the switching frequency`,`exactly at the fundamental`,`exactly at the switching frequency`,`below the fundamental`],answer:0,explain:`This passes the fundamental and blocks the switching content.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Unwanted resonance in a real circuit can cause...`,options:[`unexpectedly large voltages or currents at that frequency`,`no effect at all`,`only a change in colour of the output`,`lower switching losses automatically`],answer:0,explain:`A low-resistance resonant path can allow surprisingly large current or voltage.`}],summary:[`Resonance occurs when X_L = X_C, cancelling net reactance to zero at the resonant frequency f₀ = 1/(2π√(LC)).`,`An LC low-pass filter deliberately exploits L and C's opposite frequency behaviour to pass low frequencies and block high ones.`,`A well-placed filter cutoff sits well above the fundamental and well below the switching frequency, typically by a factor of 10 or more each way.`,`This is the mechanism behind the output filter's THD reduction in a pure-sine inverter (F5.M3.L3, Build lab project D).`],homework:{id:`F3.M2.L4.hw`,title:`Resonance and filter placement`,brief:`Compute resonant frequency and check filter placement.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Resonant frequency of a 0.5 mH, 20 µF LC combination (Hz, to the nearest whole number)?`,answer:1592,tol:.03,unit:`Hz`,explain:`f₀ = 1/(2π√(0.5e-3×20e-6)) ≈ 1592 Hz.`}],written:[{prompt:`A designer chooses an output filter with a cutoff frequency of 400 Hz for an inverter with a 50 Hz fundamental and a 20 kHz switching frequency. Evaluate this choice using the margin guideline in this lesson, and suggest an improvement if needed.`,model:`The ratio to the fundamental is 400/50 = 8×, which is below the suggested margin of at least 10×, so the fundamental may be noticeably attenuated, reducing output voltage and adding distortion. The ratio to the switching frequency (20,000/400 = 50×) is comfortably large. The designer should raise the cutoff frequency (by reducing L and/or C) to at least about 500 Hz to restore a 10× margin above the fundamental while still staying well below the switching frequency.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`X_C of a 50 µF capacitor at 50 Hz (Ω)?`,answer:63.66,tol:.02,unit:`Ω`,explain:`X_C = 1/(2π×50×50e-6) ≈ 63.66 Ω.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`As frequency rises, inductive reactance...`,options:[`rises`,`falls`,`stays constant`,`becomes resistive`],answer:0,explain:`X_L = 2πfL.`},{id:`q3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Z for R = 9 Ω, X = 12 Ω (Ω)?`,answer:15,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`√(81+144)=√225=15 Ω (3-4-5 scaled).`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`At resonance, net reactance is...`,options:[`zero`,`maximum`,`infinite`,`equal to R always`],answer:0,explain:`X_L = X_C at resonance.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An inverter output LC filter's cutoff should sit...`,options:[`well above the fundamental, well below the switching frequency`,`exactly at the fundamental`,`exactly at the switching frequency`,`at zero Hz`],answer:0,explain:`This passes the fundamental and blocks the switching content.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In an ideal capacitor, current...`,options:[`leads voltage by 90°`,`lags voltage by 90°`,`is in phase with voltage`,`is always zero`],answer:0,explain:`Current is largest when voltage changes fastest.`}]},practicals:[]},{id:`F3.M3`,title:`AC power`,summary:`Real, reactive and apparent power; power factor and why current can lag voltage; harmonics and THD as a power-quality concern; and what "energy returned" by a reactive load actually means.`,lessons:[{id:`F3.M3.L1`,title:`Real, reactive and apparent power`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Define real, reactive and apparent power and their units.`,`Compute apparent power from RMS voltage and current.`,`Explain why a load's VA rating can differ from its W rating.`,`Relate real, reactive and apparent power in the power triangle.`],introduction:`An inverter's rating in VA and its rating in W are not always the same number, and the difference is not a manufacturing quirk — it is real electrical engineering, and this lesson explains exactly what each quantity means.`,lecture:`### Three kinds of AC power

- **Apparent power (S)**, in volt-amperes (VA): S = V_rms × I_rms. This is simply the product of RMS voltage and RMS current, with no regard for phase — it is what you get by multiplying the two numbers on a meter's display.
- **Real power (P)**, in watts (W): the power actually converted to useful work or heat. P = V_rms × I_rms × cos(φ), where φ is the phase angle between voltage and current (F3.M2.L3).
- **Reactive power (Q)**, in volt-amperes reactive (VAR): power that oscillates back and forth into and out of reactive components (capacitors, inductors) without being consumed. Q = V_rms × I_rms × sin(φ).

### Why they differ

For a PURELY resistive load, voltage and current are in phase (φ = 0°), so cos(φ) = 1 and real power equals apparent power exactly: P = S, Q = 0. For a load with significant reactance (a motor, a transformer, many electronic power supplies), φ ≠ 0°, so P < S: the circuit draws more apparent power (current) than the real power it actually uses would suggest.

### The power triangle

S, P and Q relate as the three sides of a right triangle: **S² = P² + Q²**. This is the same Pythagorean relationship as impedance's Z² = R² + X² (F3.M2.L3) — not a coincidence, since power scales from the same voltage, current and phase-angle relationships.

### Why an inverter's VA rating can exceed its W rating

An inverter must be sized to handle the APPARENT power (current) a reactive load draws, even though only the real power is "useful" — this is exactly why inverter and generator nameplates often state BOTH a W and a VA rating, and why a reactive load (such as a motor) can draw its full rated VA from the inverter while delivering less real power than that VA figure might suggest.`,technical:`**Why reactive power still matters even though it does "no work".** Reactive power still requires current to flow, and current causes real I²R losses in the supply conductors, transformer windings and the inverter's own switching devices — so a load with poor power factor (high reactive power relative to real power) still costs real losses and capacity, even though the reactive power itself is not "consumed" by the load.

**Sign of reactive power.** By convention, an inductive load is said to draw (positive) reactive power, while a capacitive load is said to supply (negative) reactive power — this sign convention explains why capacitors are sometimes deliberately added to a system (power factor correction, briefly mentioned in F3.M3.L2) to offset an inductive load's reactive power demand.`,diagrams:[{id:`kirchhoff`,caption:`The power triangle: apparent power S as the hypotenuse, with real power P and reactive power Q as its two perpendicular components.`}],formulas:[{name:`Apparent power`,expr:`S = V_rms · I_rms`,where:[[`S`,`apparent power`,`VA`]]},{name:`Real power`,expr:`P = V_rms · I_rms · cos(φ)`,where:[[`P`,`real power`,`W`],[`φ`,`phase angle between voltage and current`,`°`]]},{name:`Reactive power`,expr:`Q = V_rms · I_rms · sin(φ)`,where:[[`Q`,`reactive power`,`VAR`]]},{name:`Power triangle`,expr:`S² = P² + Q²`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Apparent power of an inverter output`,problem:`An inverter supplies 230 V rms at 8.7 A rms. What is the apparent power?`,steps:[`S = V_rms × I_rms`,`= 230 × 8.7`],answer:`2001 VA ≈ 2 kVA.`},{title:`Real power with a 36.9° phase angle`,problem:`For the same 2001 VA load, the current lags voltage by 36.9° (cos 36.9° ≈ 0.8). Find the real and reactive power.`,steps:[`P = S × cos(φ) = 2001 × 0.8`,`Q = S × sin(φ) = 2001 × 0.6 (sin 36.9° ≈ 0.6)`],answer:`P ≈ 1601 W; Q ≈ 1201 VAR.`,check:`S² should equal P² + Q²: 2001² ≈ 1601² + 1201² (4,004,001 ≈ 2,563,201 + 1,442,401 = 4,005,602, consistent within rounding).`}],applications:`This is precisely why installers must check BOTH the W and VA rating of an inverter against a load list when sizing a system (S2's load-profile and system-design modules): a motor-heavy load list can demand far more VA than its headline wattage would suggest.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating VA and W as interchangeable.`,correction:`They are equal only for a purely resistive load (φ = 0°); for a reactive load, VA (apparent power) exceeds W (real power).`},{mistake:`Ignoring reactive power because "it does no work".`,correction:`Reactive power still drives real current, causing real I²R losses and consuming supply/inverter capacity.`},{mistake:`Adding P, Q and S arithmetically.`,correction:`They combine as the sides of a right triangle: S² = P² + Q², not a simple sum.`}],demo:{title:`Measure real inverter output quantities`,text:`The oscilloscope's RMS measurement and a clamp-meter current reading together give you everything needed to compute S; F3.M3.L2 adds the phase angle for P and Q.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the oscilloscope`,`scope`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.instrument(`Clamp meter`,`clamp`),e.fun(`Fundamentals: real, reactive and apparent power (fun-06)`,`fun-06`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Apparent power of 120 V rms at 15 A rms (VA)?`,answer:1800,tol:.01,unit:`VA`,explain:`S = 120×15 = 1800 VA.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`For a purely resistive load, real power and apparent power are...`,options:[`equal`,`always different`,`related by a fixed 2:1 ratio`,`unrelated`],answer:0,explain:`φ = 0° gives cos(φ) = 1, so P = S.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`S, P and Q relate as...`,options:[`S² = P² + Q²`,`S = P + Q`,`S = P − Q`,`P = Q always`],answer:0,explain:`The power triangle is a right-triangle relationship.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why does reactive power still matter even though it does no useful work?`,options:[`It still drives real current, causing real I²R losses and consuming supply/inverter capacity`,`It is purely theoretical and has no real effect`,`It only matters for DC systems`,`It cancels real power automatically`],answer:0,explain:`Reactive current is still real current flowing through real resistances.`}],summary:[`Apparent power S = V_rms×I_rms (VA); real power P = S×cos(φ) (W); reactive power Q = S×sin(φ) (VAR).`,`For a purely resistive load, P = S; for a reactive load, P < S.`,`S, P and Q combine as a right triangle: S² = P² + Q².`,`An inverter must be sized for apparent power (VA), because reactive current still flows and causes real losses, even though it does no useful work.`],homework:{id:`F3.M3.L1.hw`,title:`Power triangle practice`,brief:`Compute S, P and Q.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Apparent power of 48 V rms at 20 A rms (VA)?`,answer:960,tol:.01,unit:`VA`,explain:`S = 48×20 = 960 VA.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Real power for S = 960 VA at cos(φ) = 0.9 (W)?`,answer:864,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`P = 960×0.9 = 864 W.`}],written:[{prompt:`An inverter is rated 3000 VA / 2400 W. Explain, using the concepts in this lesson, what this pair of ratings tells an installer about the loads it can safely supply.`,model:`The 2400 W figure is the maximum REAL power the inverter can deliver as useful output; the 3000 VA figure is the maximum APPARENT power (current at rated voltage) it can supply regardless of load phase angle. This implies a minimum acceptable power factor of 2400/3000 = 0.8: if the connected loads have a lower power factor than this (more reactive relative to real power), their apparent-power (current) demand will exceed what the inverter can supply even though their real-power demand looks acceptable, and the inverter's current limit (not its wattage limit) becomes the binding constraint.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M3.L2`,title:`Power factor and current lag`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Define power factor and compute it from real and apparent power or from the phase angle.`,`Explain why an inductive load causes current to lag voltage, and what this means practically.`,`State the practical consequences of a low power factor for conductor sizing and losses.`,`Explain, qualitatively, the idea of power factor correction.`],introduction:`[[power-factor|Power factor]] packages the phase relationship between voltage and current — and therefore the gap between real and apparent power — into a single number between 0 and 1, the one figure most generator, inverter and utility documentation actually quotes.`,lecture:`### Power factor defined

**Power factor (PF) = P / S = cos(φ)**

A PF of 1.0 (unity) means the load is purely resistive: all the apparent power delivered becomes real, useful power. A PF below 1.0 means some of the apparent power is "wasted" as reactive power that still draws current but delivers no useful work. Most inductive loads (motors, transformers, many fluorescent and some switching power supplies) have a PF noticeably below 1.0, typically 0.6–0.9 depending on the device and its loading.

### Lagging versus leading power factor

- **Lagging power factor**: current lags voltage, caused by net INDUCTIVE reactance (F3.M2.L2) — the most common case for motors and transformers.
- **Leading power factor**: current leads voltage, caused by net CAPACITIVE reactance (F3.M2.L1) — less common as a natural load characteristic, but deliberately used in power factor correction.

### Practical consequences of low power factor

A load with a given real-power (W) demand but a low power factor draws MORE current than the same real power at unity power factor would require (because I = P/(V×PF), and a smaller PF means a larger required current for the same P). This larger current means:

- Larger conductors are needed to carry it without excessive voltage drop or overheating.
- Greater I²R losses in those conductors and in the inverter's own output stage.
- The inverter itself may hit its CURRENT (VA) limit before its WATTAGE limit, exactly as explored in F3.M3.L1's worked example.

### Power factor correction

Because inductive and capacitive reactances have opposite effects (F3.M2.L2), adding a correctly sized CAPACITOR in parallel with an inductive load can supply some or all of the reactive power the inductive load demands LOCALLY, reducing the reactive current the supply itself must carry — this is power factor correction, widely used in industrial installations with large motor loads. This programme introduces the concept; detailed correction-capacitor sizing is beyond its scope.`,technical:`**Displacement power factor versus true power factor.** The PF = cos(φ) relationship above is strictly correct only for a purely sinusoidal current displaced in phase from a sinusoidal voltage ("displacement power factor"). A non-sinusoidal (harmonic-rich) current, as drawn by many switching power supplies, has a TRUE power factor that also accounts for harmonic content, and can be lower than the displacement power factor alone would suggest — this links directly to F3.M3.L3's treatment of harmonics.

**Why utilities sometimes penalise low power factor.** A utility must still size its own generation, transmission and distribution equipment for the APPARENT power a customer's load draws, even though it bills primarily for real energy (kWh) — this mismatch is why some commercial/industrial tariffs include a power-factor penalty or a demand charge related to apparent power.`,diagrams:[{id:`kirchhoff`,caption:`Lagging current (inductive load) versus leading current (capacitive load), shown as a phase shift relative to voltage.`}],formulas:[{name:`Power factor`,expr:`PF = P / S = cos(φ)`,where:[[`PF`,`power factor`,`dimensionless, 0–1`]]},{name:`Current for a given real power and power factor`,expr:`I_rms = P / (V_rms · PF)`,where:[[`I_rms`,`required RMS current`,`A`]]}],examples:[{title:`Power factor from real and apparent power`,problem:`A load draws 1600 W real power and 2000 VA apparent power. Find its power factor.`,steps:[`PF = P/S`,`= 1600/2000`],answer:`PF = 0.8 (lagging, if the load is inductive, which it almost always is for a motor).`},{title:`Extra current from poor power factor`,problem:`A 230 V supply must deliver 2000 W of real power to (a) a PF = 1.0 load and (b) a PF = 0.7 load. Compare the required current.`,steps:[`(a) I = P/(V×PF) = 2000/(230×1.0) = 8.70 A`,`(b) I = 2000/(230×0.7) = 12.42 A`],answer:`The PF = 0.7 load requires about 43% more current for the SAME real power delivered — current the conductors and inverter must still carry.`,check:`12.42/8.70 ≈ 1.43, matching 1/0.7 ≈ 1.43, confirming current scales inversely with power factor for fixed real power.`}],applications:`This is exactly why a system designer checks a load list's power factor, not just its wattage, when sizing an inverter and its conductors (S2's system-design module) — a motor-heavy load list at PF 0.7–0.8 needs meaningfully more current capacity than its wattage alone implies.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Sizing conductors and inverters from real power (W) alone.`,correction:`Size from the required current, which depends on power factor: I = P/(V×PF) — a lower PF needs more current for the same real power.`},{mistake:`Assuming all loads have a power factor close to 1.0.`,correction:`Motors, transformers and many switching supplies commonly sit at PF 0.6–0.9; check the specific load.`},{mistake:`Confusing lagging and leading power factor.`,correction:`Lagging = inductive load, current behind voltage. Leading = capacitive load, current ahead of voltage.`}],demo:{title:`Relate phase angle to power factor`,text:`The LCR meter's impedance readings (F3.M2.L3) give you R and X, from which φ = arctan(X/R) and PF = cos(φ) follow directly.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the LCR meter`,`lcr`)},activities:[e.instrument(`LCR meter`,`lcr`),e.solar(`Load profile and system design`,`design`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power factor for P = 900 W, S = 1000 VA?`,answer:.9,tol:.01,unit:``,explain:`PF = 900/1000 = 0.9.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A lagging power factor is caused by...`,options:[`net inductive reactance`,`net capacitive reactance`,`purely resistive loads only`,`a fault condition`],answer:0,explain:`Inductive loads make current lag voltage.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A lower power factor, for the same real power, requires...`,options:[`more current`,`less current`,`the same current`,`no current`],answer:0,explain:`I = P/(V×PF): a smaller PF increases required current.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Power factor correction typically adds...`,options:[`a capacitor in parallel with an inductive load`,`a resistor in series`,`an inductor in parallel with a capacitive load`,`nothing; it is a software setting only`],answer:0,explain:`A parallel capacitor supplies reactive power locally, reducing what the supply must carry.`}],summary:[`Power factor PF = P/S = cos(φ), between 0 and 1; unity PF means a purely resistive load.`,`Lagging PF (current behind voltage) comes from inductive loads; leading PF comes from capacitive loads.`,`A lower power factor requires more current for the same real power, increasing conductor and inverter current demand.`,`Power factor correction adds capacitance in parallel with an inductive load to reduce the reactive current the supply must carry.`],homework:{id:`F3.M3.L2.hw`,title:`Power factor practice`,brief:`Compute power factor and required current.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power factor for P = 1200 W, S = 1500 VA?`,answer:.8,tol:.01,unit:``,explain:`PF = 1200/1500 = 0.8.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Current for P = 1200 W at 230 V, PF = 0.8 (A)?`,answer:6.52,tol:.02,unit:`A`,explain:`I = 1200/(230×0.8) ≈ 6.52 A.`}],written:[{prompt:`A customer complains that their inverter "trips on overcurrent" when running a motor rated only 1000 W, well under the inverter's 1500 W rating. Using power factor, explain a plausible cause.`,model:`The motor's real power (1000 W) may be well under the inverter's wattage rating, but motors typically have a power factor well below 1.0 (often 0.6–0.8). At a low power factor, the REQUIRED CURRENT for that real power is higher than it would be at unity power factor (I = P/(V×PF)), and the inverter's current (VA) limit — not its wattage limit — may be exceeded even though the real-power demand looks comfortably within rating. This matches F3.M3.L1's point that an inverter's VA rating, not just its W rating, must be checked against a reactive load.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M3.L3`,title:`Harmonics and THD`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain how harmonic current distorts real power calculations beyond simple displacement power factor.`,`Relate a load's harmonic current content to additional heating in supply conductors and transformers.`,`Explain why some loads specify a maximum acceptable supply THD.`],introduction:`F5.M3.L3 introduced THD as a property of an inverter's OUTPUT voltage waveform. This lesson looks at the other side: harmonic CURRENT drawn by non-linear loads (many electronic power supplies, variable-speed drives, LED drivers) and what it costs in real power terms, tying harmonics directly into this module's power calculations.`,lecture:`### Harmonic current from non-linear loads

Many modern loads (switch-mode power supplies, LED drivers, variable-frequency drives) do not draw a smooth sinusoidal current even from a perfectly sinusoidal voltage supply — they draw current in short pulses, rich in harmonics, because of their internal rectification and switching stages. This is DIFFERENT from the displacement power factor of F3.M3.L2 (a phase shift of a sinusoidal current); harmonic current distorts the CURRENT'S SHAPE itself.

### Extra heating from harmonic current

Harmonic currents still flow through supply conductors, transformers and protective devices, contributing to I²R heating exactly as the fundamental current does — even though, like reactive current, they may not represent useful delivered power at the fundamental frequency. A conductor or transformer carrying significant harmonic current can overheat even while its TOTAL RMS current appears within rating, because some standard ratings assume a largely sinusoidal current.

### True power factor with harmonics

With harmonic content present, "true power factor" (as distinct from the simpler displacement power factor of F3.M3.L2) accounts for both the phase displacement AND the harmonic distortion of the current — a load can have good displacement power factor (little phase shift) but still poor true power factor because of harmonic content.

### Why some loads specify a maximum supply THD

A small minority of sensitive loads (certain medical, audio, or precision measurement equipment) specify a maximum acceptable THD on their SUPPLY voltage, because harmonic voltage distortion can interact with their own internal circuitry in ways that degrade performance — this is the same THD concept from F5.M3.L3, now viewed as a supply-quality requirement a load can impose, rather than only a property an inverter's output might have.`,technical:`**THD on current versus THD on voltage.** F5.M3.L3 discussed output voltage THD (an inverter's own waveform quality). This lesson's harmonic CURRENT is a property of what a LOAD draws from an otherwise clean sinusoidal supply — a different, though related, phenomenon. Both are summarised by the same mathematical THD formula applied to the relevant quantity (voltage or current).

**Triplen harmonics and neutral current.** In three-phase systems (briefly noted here, beyond this course's depth), certain harmonics (odd multiples of 3: the 3rd, 9th, 15th...) can add rather than cancel in the neutral conductor, a specific concern for sizing neutral conductors on harmonic-rich loads — flagged for awareness, not required for calculation at this level.`,diagrams:[{id:`spwm`,caption:`A non-sinusoidal load current pulse train drawn from a sinusoidal voltage supply, and its harmonic spectrum.`}],formulas:[{name:`Total harmonic distortion (recap, F5.M3.L3)`,expr:`THD = √(I₂² + I₃² + …) / I₁`,where:[[`I₁`,`fundamental RMS current`,`A`],[`Iₙ`,`n-th harmonic RMS current`,`A`]],note:`Applied here to load current rather than inverter output voltage.`}],examples:[{title:`Harmonic current heating a conductor beyond its apparent rating`,problem:`A conductor rated 20 A (at a standard, largely sinusoidal current assumption) carries a total RMS current of 18 A, but a third of that RMS total is harmonic content well above the fundamental. Why might this still be a problem despite being "within rating"?`,steps:[`The conductor's 20 A rating assumes a largely sinusoidal current profile typical of resistive/linear loads`,`Significant harmonic content changes how that current distributes and heats the conductor (and nearby equipment such as transformers) differently than the rating's assumption`],answer:`A conductor or transformer carrying significant harmonic content can run hotter than its nominal RMS rating alone would predict, because the rating was not derived for a harmonic-rich current profile — this is why harmonic-heavy installations sometimes need derated or specially rated equipment.`},{title:`Why true power factor can be worse than displacement power factor`,problem:`A switch-mode load has almost no phase displacement between the fundamental component of its current and the voltage (displacement PF ≈ 0.99) but draws a strongly non-sinusoidal (harmonic-rich) current. Explain why its TRUE power factor can still be noticeably below 0.99.`,steps:[`Displacement power factor only accounts for phase shift of the fundamental`,`True power factor also accounts for the extra RMS current from harmonics, which increases S (apparent power) without increasing P (real power)`],answer:`The extra harmonic RMS current raises apparent power (S = V×I_total,rms) without delivering any extra real power, lowering true power factor (P/S) even with near-zero fundamental phase displacement.`}],applications:`This is why some installation standards limit the proportion of non-linear (harmonic-generating) load a circuit may carry, and why larger harmonic-heavy installations sometimes specify harmonic filters or derated transformers — considerations that feed into S2's system design and commissioning content.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a load's current is sinusoidal just because its voltage supply is.`,correction:`Many non-linear loads (switching supplies, LED drivers, VFDs) draw a distorted current from a clean sinusoidal supply.`},{mistake:`Treating displacement power factor as the whole picture.`,correction:`Harmonic-rich loads need true power factor, which also accounts for current distortion, not just phase shift.`},{mistake:`"If total RMS current is within rating, there is no heating concern."`,correction:`Significant harmonic content can cause extra heating beyond what a standard RMS-based rating assumes.`}],demo:{title:`Compare clean and distorted current shapes`,text:`View a clean sinusoidal current versus a pulsed, harmonic-rich current shape on the oscilloscope to see the qualitative difference this lesson describes.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the oscilloscope`,`scope`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.lib(`PWM controller`,`pwm-controller`),e.fun(`Fundamentals: harmonics, THD and filtering (fun-05)`,`fun-05`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Harmonic current is drawn by...`,options:[`non-linear loads such as switching power supplies`,`only purely resistive loads`,`only when the supply voltage is distorted`,`never, in modern equipment`],answer:0,explain:`Non-linear internal rectification/switching draws a distorted current.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`True power factor differs from displacement power factor by also accounting for...`,options:[`harmonic (current shape) distortion`,`supply frequency only`,`temperature`,`the inverter's brand`],answer:0,explain:`True PF includes both phase displacement and harmonic content.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A conductor rated for a sinusoidal current, carrying significant harmonic content at the same total RMS value, may...`,options:[`run hotter than the rating alone would predict`,`run cooler`,`be completely unaffected`,`only be affected if the frequency is below 50 Hz`],answer:0,explain:`Standard RMS ratings often assume a largely sinusoidal profile.`}],summary:[`Many non-linear loads draw harmonic-rich current even from a clean sinusoidal voltage supply.`,`Harmonic current still causes real I²R heating in conductors and transformers, without delivering extra useful power.`,`True power factor accounts for both phase displacement AND harmonic distortion; it can be noticeably worse than displacement power factor alone.`,`Some sensitive loads specify a maximum supply voltage THD, extending the THD concept from F5.M3.L3 into a supply-quality requirement.`],homework:{id:`F3.M3.L3.hw`,title:`Harmonic current reasoning`,brief:`Reason about harmonic current effects.`,tasks:[],written:[{prompt:`A facility manager says "our total current is within the transformer's rated amps, so there is no problem," despite many LED drivers and switching supplies on the circuit. Evaluate this reasoning.`,model:`Total RMS current being within rating does not fully capture the risk: if a significant fraction of that current is harmonic content, the transformer (and conductors) may run hotter than the rating, which was derived assuming a largely sinusoidal current, would predict. The facility manager should also check the installation's harmonic content (or true power factor) against the transformer's suitability for non-linear loads, not rely on total RMS amps alone.`},{prompt:`Explain, in your own words, why a load can have a displacement power factor close to 1.0 yet still have a poor true power factor.`,model:`Displacement power factor only measures the phase shift between the fundamental components of voltage and current. A load can draw current with almost no phase shift at the fundamental frequency (good displacement PF) while still drawing substantial harmonic current at other frequencies. That harmonic current adds to the total RMS current and therefore to apparent power S, without adding to real power P, which lowers the true power factor P/S even though the displacement component alone looks good.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M3.L4`,title:`Energy returned by reactive loads`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain what "energy returned" by a reactive load means physically.`,`Describe the cyclical energy exchange between a source and a reactive (capacitive or inductive) load.`,`Explain why this returned energy still requires current-carrying capacity even though it is not consumed.`],introduction:`Reactive power (F3.M3.L1) is sometimes described as energy that is "sent out and sent back" each cycle rather than consumed. This closing lesson of the module makes that description concrete: what is actually flowing, and why it still matters for sizing and losses even though none of it is "used up".`,lecture:`### The cyclical exchange

A reactive component (capacitor or inductor) stores energy during part of each AC cycle and returns essentially all of it to the source during another part of the same cycle — ideally, with no net energy consumed over a full cycle (an ideal reactance dissipates nothing; F3.M2.L1–L2). This back-and-forth energy flow is what reactive power represents: energy genuinely flowing in the circuit, but with zero net transfer to the load over a complete cycle.

### Why this still requires capacity, even with zero net transfer

Although the NET energy transferred by the reactive component over a full cycle is zero, the INSTANTANEOUS current needed to move that energy back and forth is real, physical current — it must still flow through the same conductors, switches and windings that carry the real-power current, and it still causes real I²R heating in them (F3.M3.L3 makes the same point for harmonic current). This is why reactive power, despite transferring zero net energy to the load, still consumes real current-carrying capacity and still shows up on an apparent-power (VA) rating.

### A helpful (imperfect) analogy

Imagine a cart being pushed back and forth on a frictionless track: over a full cycle, you do zero NET work on the cart (it ends up back where it started, at the same speed), yet your muscles clearly did real, physical effort throughout — this effort is the "capacity" a reactive load demands from the supply, even though its NET energy balance is zero. The analogy breaks down in detail (muscles are not lossless the way an ideal reactance is), but it captures the key idea: real activity and real net transfer are two different things.

### Tying the module together

This completes the picture begun in F3.M3.L1: real power is net energy transferred and consumed; reactive power is energy genuinely exchanged back and forth with zero net transfer, but which still occupies real current-carrying capacity; apparent power is the total current-related "activity" the supply must be sized for, combining both.`,technical:`**Instantaneous power can be negative.** For a purely reactive load, instantaneous power p(t) = v(t)×i(t) oscillates between positive (energy flowing from source to load) and negative (energy flowing from load back to source) values, averaging to exactly zero over a full cycle — this negative-power half of the cycle is the literal mathematical expression of "energy returned".

**Why a battery or inverter still experiences reactive current.** Even though an ideal reactive load returns its borrowed energy rather than consuming it, the SOURCE (battery, inverter output stage) must still supply and then re-absorb that current each cycle, which is why inverter output stages and their protective devices are rated in VA (apparent power) as well as W (real power), as established in F3.M3.L1.`,diagrams:[{id:`kirchhoff`,caption:`Instantaneous power for a reactive load oscillating between positive (source to load) and negative (load to source) over one cycle, averaging to zero.`}],formulas:[{name:`Instantaneous power`,expr:`p(t) = v(t) · i(t)`,where:[[`p(t)`,`instantaneous power`,`W`],[`v(t)`,`instantaneous voltage`,`V`],[`i(t)`,`instantaneous current`,`A`]],note:`For a purely reactive load, p(t) averages to zero over a full cycle, oscillating between positive and negative values.`}],examples:[{title:`Instantaneous power sign for a reactive load`,problem:`At one instant, a capacitor's voltage and current are both positive (charging); at a later instant, its voltage is positive but its current is negative (discharging back toward the source). What does the sign of instantaneous power tell you in each case?`,steps:[`Charging: v>0 and i>0, so p = v×i > 0 — energy flowing INTO the capacitor from the source`,`Discharging: v>0 and i<0, so p = v×i < 0 — energy flowing OUT of the capacitor, back toward the source`],answer:`Positive instantaneous power means energy is flowing from source to load at that instant; negative means it is flowing back from load to source — exactly the "sent out and sent back" picture.`},{title:`Why a protective device still sees reactive current`,problem:`A circuit breaker is rated by the current it must interrupt safely. Explain why a purely reactive load's current, despite transferring zero net energy over a cycle, must still be included when checking the breaker's rating.`,steps:[`A circuit breaker responds to the actual instantaneous/RMS current flowing through it, not to net energy transferred`,`A reactive load's current is real, physical current flowing through the breaker at every instant, even though its cycle-average energy transfer is zero`],answer:`The breaker must be rated for the full RMS current actually flowing, which includes the reactive component — "zero net energy" does not mean "zero current".`}],applications:`This is the underlying reason every rating this module has discussed — conductor sizing, inverter VA rating, breaker rating — is based on CURRENT (or apparent power), not on net energy delivered: reactive current is real current, even though its net energy transfer is zero.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`"Reactive power does nothing, so it can be ignored when sizing equipment."`,correction:`Reactive power still requires real current flow, which must be accommodated by conductor, switch and inverter current ratings.`},{mistake:`Assuming instantaneous power is always positive in an AC circuit.`,correction:`For a reactive load, instantaneous power swings negative for part of each cycle, representing energy flowing back to the source.`},{mistake:`Confusing "zero net energy transfer" with "zero current".`,correction:`A reactive load can have zero net energy transfer over a cycle while still carrying substantial instantaneous current throughout.`}],demo:{title:`See the cyclical pattern`,text:`Capture voltage and current together (conceptually, using this module's R/X/φ relationships) to see where instantaneous power would be positive and negative over a cycle.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the oscilloscope`,`scope`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.sim(`H-bridge, inductive load (energy returned to the supply)`,`sim-08`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Over a full cycle, an ideal reactive load's net energy transfer is...`,options:[`zero`,`always positive`,`always negative`,`equal to the real power`],answer:0,explain:`Energy stored is returned in full each cycle for an ideal reactance.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Negative instantaneous power in a reactive circuit means...`,options:[`energy is flowing from the load back toward the source at that instant`,`a fault has occurred`,`the meter is broken`,`the frequency has reversed`],answer:0,explain:`The sign of p(t) indicates the direction of energy flow at that instant.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why must a breaker's rating still account for reactive current?`,options:[`It responds to actual current flowing, regardless of net energy transferred`,`Reactive current never actually flows`,`Breakers ignore reactive loads by design`,`Reactive current is always safely below rated current`],answer:0,explain:`Zero net energy does not mean zero current.`}],summary:[`A reactive load stores energy during part of each cycle and returns essentially all of it to the source during another part, with zero net transfer over a full cycle.`,`Instantaneous power p(t) = v(t)×i(t) swings positive and negative for a reactive load, averaging to zero.`,`This returned energy still requires real, physical current flow through conductors, switches and the inverter's output stage.`,`This is why conductor, breaker and inverter ratings are based on current/apparent power, not on net energy delivered.`],homework:{id:`F3.M3.L4.hw`,title:`Reactive energy exchange`,brief:`Reason about instantaneous power sign and current rating.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`A purely reactive load draws 15 A rms from a 230 V rms supply. What apparent power (VA) must the supply conductors be rated for, even though real power delivered is zero?`,answer:3450,tol:.01,unit:`VA`,explain:`S = V_rms×I_rms = 230×15 = 3450 VA, regardless of the zero real power.`}],written:[{prompt:`A student argues that a large bank of capacitors connected to a 230 V supply with no resistive load at all "uses no power at all, so it needs no current rating on its supply conductors." Evaluate this claim fully, using the ideas from this lesson.`,model:`It is true that an ideal capacitor bank transfers zero NET energy over a full cycle — it stores and returns energy each cycle without net consumption. However, the student's conclusion that it "needs no current rating" is wrong: moving that energy back and forth each cycle requires real, physical current (reactive current) to flow through the supply conductors at every instant, and this current causes real I²R heating in those conductors regardless of the zero net energy transfer. The supply conductors must still be rated for the actual RMS current the capacitor bank draws, exactly as if it were doing useful work.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Apparent power of 230 V rms at 10 A rms (VA)?`,answer:2300,tol:.01,unit:`VA`,explain:`S = 230×10 = 2300 VA.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`For a purely resistive load, P and S are...`,options:[`equal`,`always different`,`related by a fixed ratio`,`unrelated`],answer:0,explain:`cos(0°) = 1.`},{id:`q3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power factor for P = 1500 W, S = 2000 VA?`,answer:.75,tol:.01,unit:``,explain:`PF = 1500/2000 = 0.75.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A lagging power factor is caused by...`,options:[`net inductive reactance`,`net capacitive reactance`,`purely resistive loads`,`a fault`],answer:0,explain:`Inductive loads make current lag voltage.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`True power factor differs from displacement power factor by also including...`,options:[`harmonic current distortion`,`supply frequency`,`ambient temperature`,`the meter brand`],answer:0,explain:`True PF accounts for both phase shift and harmonic content.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Over a full cycle, an ideal reactive load's net energy transfer is...`,options:[`zero`,`positive`,`negative`,`equal to apparent power`],answer:0,explain:`Energy is stored and returned each cycle.`}]},practicals:[]},{id:`F3.M4`,title:`Transformers`,summary:`Mutual induction and turns ratio, ideal versus real transformer behaviour, isolation and safety, and the main transformer connection types.`,lessons:[{id:`F3.M4.L1`,title:`Mutual induction and turns ratio`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain mutual induction as the physical basis of transformer action.`,`State and apply the turns-ratio relationship between primary and secondary voltage.`,`Explain how transformer current relates inversely to the turns ratio.`,`Relate this to F3.M1.L1's claim that transformers require AC.`],introduction:`F3.M1.L1 stated that transformers require AC because they rely on a changing magnetic flux. This lesson derives exactly how that changing flux links two separate windings and produces the turns-ratio relationship behind every step-up and step-down transformer you will meet.`,lecture:`### Mutual induction

Two coils of wire, wound around a common magnetic core, are linked by the same magnetic flux Φ passing through both. When the current in one coil (the **primary**) changes, so does the flux it produces, and — by Faraday's law — this changing flux induces a voltage in the SECOND coil (the **secondary**), even though the two coils have no direct electrical connection. This is **mutual induction**, and it is the entire physical basis of transformer action.

### Turns ratio and voltage

Each winding's induced voltage is proportional to its number of turns (N), because each turn links the same changing flux and contributes its own small induced voltage, which add in series along the winding:

**V_s / V_p = N_s / N_p**

A transformer with MORE secondary turns than primary turns (N_s > N_p) is a **step-up** transformer (V_s > V_p); fewer secondary turns gives a **step-down** transformer (V_s < V_p).

### Turns ratio and current (ideal transformer)

For an IDEAL transformer (no losses), input and output apparent power are equal: V_p·I_p = V_s·I_s. Combined with the voltage turns-ratio relationship, this gives current in the OPPOSITE ratio to voltage:

**I_s / I_p = N_p / N_s**

A step-up transformer (higher secondary voltage) therefore has LOWER secondary current than primary current, for the same transferred power — exactly why high-voltage transmission lines (F3.M1.L1) can use comparatively thin conductors for a given power.

### Why this needs AC (tying back to F3.M1.L1)

Mutual induction depends entirely on a CHANGING flux. A steady DC primary current produces a steady flux once established, inducing no ongoing secondary voltage — this lesson's physics is exactly what F3.M1.L1 asserted without deriving.`,technical:`**The magnetising current and core.** A real transformer's primary draws a small "magnetising current" even with no secondary load, needed to establish the core's magnetic flux; this is one of the real-transformer imperfections examined in F3.M4.L2. The core itself (laminated iron, or ferrite at higher frequencies) is chosen to carry the flux efficiently with low loss — core material and construction are a significant transformer design topic beyond this lesson's scope.

**Transformers at switching frequency.** The high-frequency transformers sometimes used inside inverter power stages (as opposed to a 50/60 Hz mains transformer) rely on exactly the same turns-ratio physics, but use a much smaller, lighter core because mutual induction is more efficient at higher frequency for a given core size — a design trade-off noted here for context, not required for calculation at this level.`,diagrams:[{id:`hf-link`,caption:`Primary and secondary windings linked by a common magnetic core and flux, with the turns-ratio relationship between V_p, V_s, N_p and N_s.`}],formulas:[{name:`Voltage turns ratio`,expr:`V_s / V_p = N_s / N_p`,where:[[`V_p, V_s`,`primary and secondary voltage`,`V`],[`N_p, N_s`,`primary and secondary turns`,`turns`]]},{name:`Current turns ratio (ideal)`,expr:`I_s / I_p = N_p / N_s`,where:[[`I_p, I_s`,`primary and secondary current`,`A`]]}],examples:[{title:`Step-down transformer voltage`,problem:`A transformer has 1000 primary turns and 50 secondary turns, with 230 V applied to the primary. Find the secondary voltage.`,steps:[`V_s = V_p × (N_s/N_p)`,`= 230 × (50/1000)`],answer:`11.5 V.`},{title:`Secondary current for the same transformer`,problem:`For the transformer in the previous example, the secondary load draws 4 A. Find the (ideal) primary current.`,steps:[`I_p = I_s × (N_s/N_p) = 4 × (50/1000)`,`Check with apparent power: S_p = V_p×I_p = 230×0.2 = 46 VA; S_s = V_s×I_s = 11.5×4 = 46 VA`],answer:`I_p = 0.2 A, and both sides' apparent power match at 46 VA, confirming the ideal-transformer power balance.`,check:`46 VA = 46 VA on both sides, consistent with lossless power transfer.`}],applications:`This is exactly how a mains isolation transformer (F3.M4.L3) or a battery-charging transformer steps 230 V or 120 V mains down to a safer or more usable secondary voltage, and it is the same turns-ratio principle behind the high-voltage step-up transformers used at power stations (F3.M1.L1).`,mistakes:[{mistake:`"A transformer increases power."`,correction:`An ideal transformer transfers power unchanged (V_p×I_p = V_s×I_s); it changes voltage and current, not power.`},{mistake:`Assuming secondary current scales the same way as secondary voltage.`,correction:`Current scales in the OPPOSITE ratio to voltage: a step-up transformer has lower secondary current, not higher.`},{mistake:`Believing a transformer works on DC if the DC is "strong enough".`,correction:`Mutual induction requires a CHANGING flux; a steady DC current, however large, induces no ongoing secondary voltage once established.`}],demo:{title:`See transformer inductance values`,text:`The LCR meter's inductance readings on transformer-style components relate directly to the turns and core properties behind this lesson's formulas.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the LCR meter`,`lcr`)},activities:[e.instrument(`LCR meter`,`lcr`),e.lib(`Transformer`,`transformer`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Secondary voltage for N_p=800, N_s=40, V_p=230 V (V)?`,answer:11.5,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`V_s = 230×(40/800) = 11.5 V.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A step-up transformer has...`,options:[`more secondary turns than primary turns`,`fewer secondary turns than primary turns`,`equal turns always`,`no core`],answer:0,explain:`N_s > N_p gives V_s > V_p.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`For an ideal transformer, secondary current relative to primary current, on a step-up transformer, is...`,options:[`lower`,`higher`,`equal`,`undefined`],answer:0,explain:`Current scales in the opposite ratio to voltage.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Mutual induction requires...`,options:[`a changing magnetic flux`,`a steady DC flux`,`no magnetic core at all`,`direct electrical connection between windings`],answer:0,explain:`Only a changing flux induces an ongoing voltage.`}],summary:[`Mutual induction links primary and secondary windings through a shared, changing magnetic flux — the physical basis of transformer action.`,`Voltage turns ratio: V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p.`,`Current turns ratio (ideal transformer): I_s/I_p = N_p/N_s — the opposite ratio from voltage, since power is conserved.`,`This changing-flux requirement is exactly why transformers need AC, as F3.M1.L1 asserted.`],homework:{id:`F3.M4.L1.hw`,title:`Turns ratio practice`,brief:`Apply the voltage and current turns-ratio relationships.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`V_s for N_p=500, N_s=25, V_p=120 V (V)?`,answer:6,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`V_s=120×(25/500)=6 V.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`I_p for N_p=500, N_s=25, I_s=10 A (A)?`,answer:.5,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`I_p=10×(25/500)=0.5 A.`}],written:[{prompt:`A step-down transformer's secondary is short-circuited by accident. Using the turns-ratio relationship between primary and secondary current, explain what happens to the primary current, and why this matters for protection (fusing/breaker sizing).`,model:`A short circuit drives the secondary current I_s toward a very large value (limited mainly by winding resistance). Because I_p = I_s×(N_s/N_p), the primary current rises in the same proportion, so the primary also sees a greatly increased current, even though the fault is physically on the secondary side. This is why primary-side (and sometimes also secondary-side) protective devices must be sized to detect and clear a secondary fault promptly — the fault's effect is not confined to the secondary winding alone.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M4.L2`,title:`Ideal and real transformers`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`List the assumptions of an ideal transformer.`,`Identify the main loss mechanisms in a real transformer: copper loss, core (iron) loss and leakage reactance.`,`Explain why transformer efficiency varies with load.`,`Relate these losses to the LCR meter's ESR/DCR measurements (F5.M4.L2).`],introduction:`F3.M4.L1's turns-ratio formulas assume an IDEAL transformer with no losses. Real transformers fall short of this ideal in specific, well-understood ways — and recognising each loss mechanism is exactly what F5.M4.L2's inductor/transformer DCR test was designed to probe.`,lecture:`### Assumptions of an ideal transformer

An ideal transformer assumes: zero winding resistance, no core losses, perfect magnetic coupling between windings (all flux links both), and no magnetising current needed. None of these hold exactly in a real transformer.

### Copper loss (I²R loss)

Real windings have resistance (their DCR, as measured in F5.M4.L2), so current flowing through them dissipates power as heat: **P_copper = I_p²R_p + I_s²R_s**. Copper loss RISES with the square of load current, so it is small at light load and significant at or above rated load.

### Core (iron) loss

The core itself loses energy on every AC cycle through two mechanisms: **hysteresis loss** (energy lost magnetising and demagnetising the core material each cycle) and **eddy current loss** (small circulating currents induced within the core material itself by the changing flux, which the core's lamination — thin, insulated sheets rather than one solid block — is specifically designed to reduce). Core loss is roughly CONSTANT regardless of load, because it depends on the applied voltage and frequency, not on load current.

### Leakage reactance

Not all of the flux produced by the primary links the secondary perfectly — some "leaks" without contributing to mutual induction. This imperfect coupling behaves like a small series inductance (leakage reactance) in each winding, causing some voltage drop under load beyond what resistance alone would cause.

### Why efficiency varies with load

Total loss = copper loss (rises with load²) + core loss (roughly constant). At very light load, core loss dominates relative to the small amount of power being delivered, so efficiency is poor. At very heavy load (or overload), rapidly rising copper loss dominates. Peak efficiency typically occurs somewhere in between, where the two loss mechanisms are roughly balanced — a transformer specified for a particular typical load is usually designed so this peak falls near that load point.`,technical:`**Relating this to F5.M4.L2's instrument work.** The LCR meter's DCR (DC resistance) check on an inductor or transformer winding directly measures the resistance responsible for copper loss; its ESR reading on a capacitor is the analogous concept for that component. This course now supplies the physical reasoning (I²R copper loss) behind why that single DCR number matters for efficiency, not just for a pass/fail health check.

**Transformer efficiency formula.** η = P_output / (P_output + P_copper + P_core), expressed as a percentage. Well-designed mains transformers commonly achieve efficiencies in the mid-90s to high-90s percent range at their rated load; this programme does not require memorising specific efficiency figures, only the loss mechanisms behind the formula.`,diagrams:[{id:`hf-link`,caption:`A real transformer's equivalent circuit showing winding resistance (copper loss), leakage reactance and a core-loss/magnetising branch, alongside the ideal turns-ratio core.`}],formulas:[{name:`Copper loss`,expr:`P_copper = I_p²R_p + I_s²R_s`,where:[[`R_p, R_s`,`primary and secondary winding resistance`,`Ω`]]},{name:`Transformer efficiency`,expr:`η = P_out / (P_out + P_copper + P_core) × 100%`,where:[[`η`,`efficiency`,`%`]]}],examples:[{title:`Copper loss at two different load levels`,problem:`A transformer's secondary resistance is 0.05 Ω. Compare copper loss at (a) 10 A and (b) 20 A secondary current (ignore primary-side loss for simplicity).`,steps:[`(a) P = I²R = 10² × 0.05 = 5 W`,`(b) P = 20² × 0.05 = 20 W`],answer:`Doubling the current quadruples the copper loss (5 W → 20 W), consistent with loss scaling as I².`,check:`(20/10)² = 4, matching the 20/5 = 4× increase in loss.`},{title:`Why a lightly loaded transformer has poor efficiency`,problem:`A transformer has a constant core loss of 8 W, and delivers only 40 W of output at light load with negligible copper loss at this low current. Find its efficiency at this operating point, and explain why it differs from a heavily loaded case.`,steps:[`η = P_out/(P_out + P_core) ×100%`,`= 40/(40+8) ×100%`],answer:`≈ 83.3% — noticeably worse than the mid-90s to high-90s percent efficiency typical nearer rated load, because the roughly constant 8 W core loss is a large fraction of the small 40 W delivered at this light load.`}],applications:`This is the exact reasoning behind sizing a transformer appropriately for its typical load (neither oversized, which wastes core loss at light load, nor undersized, which incurs excessive copper loss and heating near or above rated load) in solar and battery system design (S2).`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a real transformer has zero loss, like the ideal model.`,correction:`Real transformers have copper loss, core loss and leakage reactance; the ideal model is a simplifying approximation.`},{mistake:`Assuming core loss scales with load current the way copper loss does.`,correction:`Core loss is roughly constant with load (depends on voltage/frequency); copper loss rises with load current squared.`},{mistake:`"A bigger transformer than needed is always better, since it has more margin."`,correction:`An oversized, lightly loaded transformer suffers relatively poor efficiency because its roughly constant core loss is large relative to the small load it is actually delivering.`}],demo:{title:`Measure winding resistance directly`,text:`Use the LCR meter's DCR check on an inductor/transformer case to see the real resistance responsible for copper loss.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the LCR meter`,`lcr`)},activities:[e.instrument(`LCR meter`,`lcr`),e.solar(`System sizing and efficiency`,`design`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Copper loss in a transformer winding scales with...`,options:[`the square of load current`,`load current directly (linearly)`,`frequency only`,`core material only`],answer:0,explain:`P = I²R.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Core (iron) loss is roughly...`,options:[`constant regardless of load current`,`proportional to load current squared`,`zero at all times`,`only present at DC`],answer:0,explain:`Core loss depends mainly on applied voltage and frequency.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Lamination of a transformer core is used to reduce...`,options:[`eddy current loss`,`copper loss`,`leakage reactance entirely`,`the turns ratio`],answer:0,explain:`Thin insulated laminations limit circulating eddy currents within the core.`},{id:`k4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Copper loss for I=15 A, R=0.02 Ω (W)?`,answer:4.5,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P = 15²×0.02 = 4.5 W.`}],summary:[`An ideal transformer assumes zero winding resistance, no core loss and perfect magnetic coupling; real transformers fall short in specific ways.`,`Copper loss (I²R in the windings) rises with the square of load current.`,`Core loss (hysteresis plus eddy current loss) is roughly constant regardless of load, depending mainly on applied voltage and frequency.`,`Efficiency typically peaks where copper and core loss are roughly balanced; an oversized, lightly loaded transformer has relatively poor efficiency.`],homework:{id:`F3.M4.L2.hw`,title:`Transformer loss practice`,brief:`Compute copper loss and efficiency.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Copper loss for I=25 A, R=0.03 Ω (W)?`,answer:18.75,tol:.02,unit:`W`,explain:`P=25²×0.03=18.75 W.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Efficiency for P_out=500 W, P_copper=15 W, P_core=10 W (%, to 1 decimal)?`,answer:95.2,tol:.02,unit:`%`,explain:`η=500/(500+15+10)×100 ≈ 95.2%.`}],written:[{prompt:`A battery-based inverter system is specified with a transformer rated far above the typical daily load it will actually carry, "to be safe". Using this lesson's loss mechanisms, evaluate this design choice.`,model:`An oversized transformer running well below its rated load still incurs its roughly constant core loss, which becomes a larger fraction of the (smaller) actual power delivered, lowering day-to-day efficiency compared with a transformer sized closer to the typical load. While some headroom above typical load is reasonable for occasional peaks, choosing a transformer far larger than needed "to be safe" trades away efficiency for margin that is rarely used — a transformer sized closer to the expected typical and peak load range would likely perform better in practice.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M4.L3`,title:`Isolation and safety`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Explain how a transformer provides electrical isolation between primary and secondary circuits.`,`Explain the safety benefit of isolation in the context of insulation testing (F5.M4.L1).`,`Distinguish an isolation transformer from an autotransformer in terms of safety.`],introduction:`A transformer's secondary winding has NO direct electrical connection to its primary — only a magnetic link. This isolation is not an incidental side-effect; it is a deliberate and valuable safety property, and it is exactly why F5.M4.L1's insulation resistance tester checks for breakdown of this very separation.`,lecture:`### What isolation means

Because the primary and secondary windings of a transformer are magnetically, not electrically, coupled, a transformer can transfer power across a complete electrical break: no conductive path exists between the two sides under normal, healthy operation. This is **galvanic isolation**.

### Why isolation matters for safety

Isolation means a fault (such as a live conductor contacting the equipment's enclosure) on one side does NOT automatically create a shock hazard that connects back through the supply on the other side, because there is no conductive path between them. This is one reason isolation transformers are specified for certain hazardous environments (medical equipment, test benches working on live equipment, portable tools used outdoors) — they break a potential fault path between the mains supply and the equipment side.

### Isolation breakdown and insulation testing

The isolation barrier between windings relies on the quality of the INSULATION separating them. If that insulation degrades (moisture ingress, age, physical damage, overheating), a conductive path can form between primary and secondary, defeating the isolation and creating exactly the shock hazard isolation was meant to prevent. This is precisely what F5.M4.L1's insulation resistance tester is designed to detect — a healthy transformer should show very high insulation resistance (megohms or more) between windings; a low reading signals isolation breakdown.

### Isolation transformer versus autotransformer

An **autotransformer** uses a SINGLE winding with a tapped connection to achieve a voltage ratio, which means there IS a direct electrical (conductive) connection between its "primary" and "secondary" sides — it provides voltage transformation WITHOUT isolation. A true **isolation transformer** uses two entirely separate windings specifically to preserve the no-direct-connection safety property. The two are not interchangeable where isolation is a safety requirement, even though both can change voltage.`,technical:`**Isolation and the inverter's own output.** Some inverter designs include an internal isolation transformer on the output stage specifically to provide galvanic separation between the DC source (battery/solar array) side and the AC output side — a design choice with real safety implications for installation and fault protection, distinct from inverter designs that are transformerless (and therefore not inherently isolated) for efficiency or size reasons.

**Common-mode versus isolated measurement.** When using the oscilloscope or DMM to probe across an isolation barrier (one probe on the primary side, one on the secondary side), extra care is needed because the two sides may be at very different potentials relative to a shared reference (such as a protective earth) — a practical safety point connecting this lesson directly to the DMM/scope safety practices covered in F5.M1.L2.`,diagrams:[{id:`isolation-flow`,caption:`An isolation transformer's two separate windings with no direct conductive path, compared with an autotransformer's single tapped winding with a direct connection.`}],formulas:[{name:`Insulation resistance pass check (recap, F5.M4.L1)`,expr:`R_insulation ≥ R_min`,where:[[`R_insulation`,`measured insulation resistance between windings`,`MΩ`],[`R_min`,`minimum acceptable value for the test voltage used`,`MΩ`]],note:`A reading at or above the guideline minimum indicates the isolation barrier is intact; a low reading signals breakdown.`}],examples:[{title:`Why an isolation transformer protects against a specific fault path`,problem:`A tool's internal live conductor contacts its metal casing (an insulation fault) while the tool is powered through an isolation transformer. Explain why a person touching the casing and an earthed object is at lower risk than without the isolation transformer.`,steps:[`Without isolation: the casing becomes connected, through the fault, back to the mains supply's own earth reference, so a person forms a conductive path to earth through their body`,`With isolation: the secondary side has no direct conductive connection to the mains earth reference, so the same casing fault does not automatically create a path back to earth through the person`],answer:`The isolation transformer removes the direct conductive link back to the supply's earth reference, reducing (though not eliminating, since other fault paths can exist) the shock risk from this particular single fault.`},{title:`Why insulation breakdown defeats isolation`,problem:`An isolation transformer's winding insulation degrades until a low-resistance path exists between primary and secondary. Explain why this is now functionally similar to an autotransformer, from a safety standpoint.`,steps:[`Isolation's safety benefit depends on there being NO conductive path between primary and secondary`,`Degraded insulation creates exactly such a conductive path, even though the transformer was designed as two separate windings`],answer:`Once insulation breaks down enough to create a conductive path, the transformer no longer provides the galvanic isolation it was designed for, regardless of its original two-winding construction — which is why periodic insulation resistance testing (F5.M4.L1) matters even for equipment that was isolated when new.`}],applications:`This is exactly why installation and maintenance standards call for periodic insulation resistance testing (F5.M4.L1) on isolation transformers and similar equipment — isolation is a property that must be VERIFIED periodically, not simply assumed from the original design.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming any transformer provides isolation.`,correction:`An autotransformer, despite changing voltage, has a direct electrical connection between its sides and does NOT provide isolation; only a two-separate-winding isolation transformer does.`},{mistake:`"Isolation makes a circuit completely safe regardless of other faults."`,correction:`Isolation addresses one specific fault path; other hazards (double faults, high voltage itself) still require their own protective measures.`},{mistake:`Assuming isolation, once provided by design, remains perfect indefinitely.`,correction:`Insulation can degrade over time; periodic insulation resistance testing verifies isolation is still intact.`}],demo:{title:`Revisit the insulation tester`,text:`The safety-procedure-gated insulation resistance tester in the Virtual Instruments lab is testing exactly the isolation barrier this lesson describes.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the insulation resistance tester`,`insulation`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Insulation resistance tester`,`insulation`),e.lib(`Transformer`,`transformer`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Galvanic isolation means...`,options:[`no direct conductive (electrical) path exists between primary and secondary`,`the voltage ratio is always 1:1`,`the transformer has no core`,`the secondary has no load`],answer:0,explain:`Only a magnetic link exists between the windings.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An autotransformer differs from an isolation transformer because it...`,options:[`has a direct electrical connection between its sides via a single tapped winding`,`cannot change voltage at all`,`has two entirely separate windings`,`is always more expensive`],answer:0,explain:`A single tapped winding provides no isolation.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Insulation resistance testing on a transformer checks...`,options:[`whether the isolation barrier between windings is still intact`,`the turns ratio`,`the core loss`,`the output frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Degraded insulation can create an unwanted conductive path, defeating isolation.`}],summary:[`A transformer's windings are magnetically, not electrically, linked — this galvanic isolation is a deliberate safety property.`,`Isolation removes a specific conductive fault path back to the supply, reducing (not eliminating) certain shock risks.`,`An autotransformer provides voltage transformation without isolation, via a direct electrical connection between its sides.`,`Insulation resistance testing (F5.M4.L1) verifies that an isolation barrier remains intact over time, since insulation can degrade.`],homework:{id:`F3.M4.L3.hw`,title:`Isolation reasoning`,brief:`Reason about isolation and safety.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A transformer under test shows an insulation resistance of 0.3 MΩ between windings, well below the guideline minimum. This most likely indicates...`,options:[`isolation breakdown and a possible shock hazard`,`perfectly healthy isolation`,`an oversized core`,`a step-up winding ratio`],answer:0,explain:`A low insulation resistance reading signals degraded insulation between the windings.`}],written:[{prompt:`A maintenance technician proposes replacing a damaged isolation transformer with an autotransformer of the same voltage ratio, "since it does the same job". Evaluate this proposal from a safety standpoint.`,model:`An autotransformer and an isolation transformer can indeed produce the same voltage ratio, but they are not equivalent from a safety standpoint: the autotransformer has a direct electrical connection between its input and output sides, providing no galvanic isolation, while the original isolation transformer specifically protected against certain fault paths by having no such connection. If the original equipment was specified with an isolation transformer for safety reasons (as is common for medical equipment, test benches, or portable tools used in harsh environments), substituting an autotransformer would remove that safety property even though the voltage output looks the same, and the proposal should be rejected unless isolation is confirmed not to be a requirement for this specific application.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M4.L4`,title:`Transformer connection`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Describe common single-phase transformer winding configurations: simple, centre-tapped, and multiple secondaries.`,`Explain the purpose of a centre-tapped secondary.`,`Explain, at an introductory level, why three-phase transformers use star/delta connections.`],introduction:`Beyond the simple one-primary, one-secondary transformer of earlier lessons, real transformers are built with a variety of winding configurations to suit particular applications — this closing lesson surveys the main ones you are likely to encounter.`,lecture:`### Centre-tapped secondary

A **centre-tapped** secondary winding has an additional connection brought out from the electrical MIDPOINT of the winding, in addition to its two ends. This gives three usable terminals instead of two, and two equal half-voltages referenced to the centre tap (e.g. a 24 V centre-tapped secondary gives +12 V and −12 V relative to the centre tap, as well as the full 24 V across the two ends). Centre-tapped secondaries are commonly used to derive a dual-polarity supply (+V and −V relative to a common reference) and in certain rectifier topologies.

### Multiple secondaries

A single transformer core can carry more than one separate secondary winding, each delivering its own voltage (set by its own turns count) and isolated from the others as well as from the primary — useful where a piece of equipment needs several different voltage rails from one transformer rather than several separate transformers.

### Three-phase transformer connections (introductory)

Larger power systems use THREE-PHASE supplies (three AC voltages of the same frequency and magnitude, spaced 120° apart in phase — a direct extension of this module's phase-difference concept, F3.M1.L2). Three-phase transformers connect their windings in one of two basic patterns:

- **Star (wye)**: each winding's one end connects to a common neutral point, with the other end brought out as a phase terminal — giving access to both a phase-to-neutral and a phase-to-phase voltage.
- **Delta**: windings are connected end-to-end in a closed loop, with phase terminals brought out from the junctions — no neutral point is available directly from a delta winding.

Different combinations (star-star, star-delta, delta-star, delta-delta) on the primary and secondary sides are chosen for reasons including neutral availability, fault behaviour and harmonic circulation — a substantial topic in its own right, introduced here only so the terms are recognisable; detailed three-phase transformer design is beyond this course's scope.`,technical:`**Why star-delta combinations are common.** A star-delta transformer (star primary, delta secondary, or vice versa) is often chosen because the delta winding provides a closed path for certain harmonic currents (notably triplen harmonics, briefly flagged in F3.M3.L3) to circulate within the transformer rather than flowing out onto the wider network — a practical benefit beyond simply setting the voltage ratio.

**This programme's inverter focus.** Most of the inverter equipment in the Build Your Own Inverter laboratory is single-phase; three-phase transformer connections are included here for recognition and vocabulary, since larger commercial/industrial inverter installations (introduced at a survey level elsewhere in this programme) do use three-phase equipment.`,diagrams:[{id:`hf-link`,caption:`A centre-tapped secondary winding alongside star and delta three-phase winding connection patterns.`}],formulas:[{name:`Centre-tap half-voltage`,expr:`V_half = V_end-to-end / 2`,where:[[`V_half`,`voltage from either end to the centre tap`,`V`],[`V_end-to-end`,`voltage across the full winding`,`V`]]}],examples:[{title:`Voltages available from a centre-tapped secondary`,problem:`A transformer has a centre-tapped secondary rated 36 V end-to-end. What voltages are available relative to the centre tap, and end-to-end?`,steps:[`End-to-end: 36 V (the full winding)`,`Each half, relative to the centre tap: 36/2 = 18 V`],answer:`+18 V and −18 V relative to the centre tap, plus the full 36 V across the two ends.`},{title:`Why a delta winding has no neutral point`,problem:`Explain why a delta-connected three-phase winding cannot directly provide a neutral conductor, while a star-connected winding can.`,steps:[`A star connection joins one end of each of the three windings together at a single common point — this common point is the neutral`,`A delta connection joins windings end-to-end in a closed loop, with phase terminals at the junctions — no single common point exists to serve as a neutral`],answer:`Only the star connection has a natural common point (where the three windings meet) to bring out as a neutral conductor; a delta winding has no such point.`}],applications:`Centre-tapped secondaries are directly relevant to several rectifier and dual-supply topologies you will build in F3.M5 and the Build Your Own Inverter laboratory; three-phase connections become relevant for larger commercial installations covered at a survey level elsewhere in the broader curriculum.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a centre tap doubles the available voltage.`,correction:`A centre tap splits the SAME end-to-end voltage into two equal halves relative to the tap; it does not add voltage.`},{mistake:`Assuming a delta connection provides a neutral conductor.`,correction:`Only a star connection has a common point available to serve as a neutral; delta does not.`},{mistake:`Treating three-phase transformer connections as simply "three single-phase transformers with no further considerations".`,correction:`The choice of star/delta combination affects neutral availability, fault behaviour and harmonic circulation, not just the voltage ratio.`}],demo:{title:`Measure a transformer winding's inductance`,text:`The LCR meter can measure the inductance of each winding or half-winding of a centre-tapped transformer case independently.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the LCR meter`,`lcr`)},activities:[e.instrument(`LCR meter`,`lcr`),e.ckt(`Transformer connection exercise`,`ckt-07`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A centre-tapped secondary provides...`,options:[`an additional connection at the winding's electrical midpoint, giving two equal half-voltages`,`twice the end-to-end voltage`,`isolation between its two halves`,`a built-in rectifier`],answer:0,explain:`It splits the same total voltage into two equal halves relative to the tap.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A star (wye) winding connection provides a natural point for...`,options:[`a neutral conductor`,`a delta loop`,`isolation`,`a centre tap`],answer:0,explain:`The common junction of the three windings serves as the neutral.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A delta winding connection...`,options:[`has no natural neutral point`,`always provides a neutral`,`is the same as a centre tap`,`cannot carry three-phase power`],answer:0,explain:`Windings are joined end-to-end in a loop, with no common point.`}],summary:[`A centre-tapped secondary brings out a connection at the winding's midpoint, giving two equal half-voltages plus the full end-to-end voltage.`,`A transformer core can carry multiple isolated secondary windings, each with its own voltage.`,`Three-phase transformers use star (wye) or delta winding connections; star provides a natural neutral point, delta does not.`,`The choice of star/delta combination affects neutral availability, fault behaviour and harmonic circulation, a topic introduced here at survey level.`],homework:{id:`F3.M4.L4.hw`,title:`Transformer connections practice`,brief:`Apply centre-tap and star/delta concepts.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Half-voltage relative to the centre tap for a 48 V end-to-end centre-tapped secondary (V)?`,answer:24,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`48/2 = 24 V.`}],written:[{prompt:`A designer needs both +15 V and −15 V DC rails, referenced to a common ground, from a single AC transformer secondary feeding a rectifier stage. Explain how a centre-tapped secondary would help achieve this, in terms of this lesson's concepts.`,model:`A centre-tapped secondary naturally provides two equal half-voltages (here, AC equivalents of the eventual ±15 V) on either side of the centre tap, which can be used as the common reference (ground) point. Feeding each half separately into its own rectifier stage referenced to the centre tap produces a positive DC rail from one half and a negative DC rail from the other, both referenced to the same common point — exactly the dual-polarity supply this lesson identified as a typical use of a centre-tapped secondary.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`V_s for N_p=600, N_s=30, V_p=230 V (V)?`,answer:11.5,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`V_s=230×(30/600)=11.5 V.`},{id:`q2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Copper loss scales with...`,options:[`the square of load current`,`load current linearly`,`frequency only`,`voltage only`],answer:0,explain:`P=I²R.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Core loss is roughly...`,options:[`constant regardless of load`,`proportional to load squared`,`zero always`,`only present at light load`],answer:0,explain:`Depends mainly on applied voltage and frequency.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Galvanic isolation means...`,options:[`no direct conductive path between primary and secondary`,`a 1:1 voltage ratio`,`no magnetic core`,`no load connected`],answer:0,explain:`Only a magnetic link exists.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An autotransformer provides voltage transformation...`,options:[`without isolation, via a direct electrical connection`,`with full isolation`,`only at DC`,`only for three-phase systems`],answer:0,explain:`A single tapped winding has a direct connection.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A star winding connection provides a natural point for...`,options:[`a neutral conductor`,`a centre tap`,`core lamination`,`leakage reactance`],answer:0,explain:`The common junction of the three windings.`}]},practicals:[]},{id:`F3.M5`,title:`Rectification`,summary:`Half-wave and full-wave rectification, the bridge rectifier topology, and smoothing with ripple analysis — the AC-to-DC conversion this course set up as the opposite of inversion.`,lessons:[{id:`F3.M5.L1`,title:`Half-wave and full-wave rectification`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain how a single diode produces half-wave rectification.`,`Explain how a full-wave rectifier uses both halves of the AC cycle.`,`Compare the average (DC) output and ripple frequency of half-wave and full-wave rectification.`,`Relate rectifier diode behaviour to the diode test covered in F5.M2.L1.`],introduction:`F3.M1.L4 flagged rectification as the reverse of inversion: converting AC back to DC. This lesson builds that conversion from its simplest component — a single diode — up to the more practical full-wave arrangement, directly using the diode behaviour you tested with the DMM in F5.M2.L1.`,lecture:`### The diode as a one-way valve

A [[diode|diode]] conducts current in one direction (forward) with a small voltage drop, and blocks it almost entirely in the other (reverse) — exactly the behaviour F5.M2.L1's diode test mode measured directly. This one-way property is the entire basis of rectification.

### Half-wave rectification

A single diode placed in series with an AC source and a load conducts only during the half of each cycle where the source polarity forwards-biases it, and blocks during the other half. The result is a pulsed, ALL-POSITIVE (or all-negative, depending on diode orientation) output — current flows for only half of each cycle, with the output sitting at zero for the other half.

### Full-wave rectification

A full-wave rectifier uses additional diodes (either a centre-tapped transformer secondary with two diodes, or four diodes in a bridge arrangement, F3.M5.L2) so that BOTH halves of the AC cycle contribute current to the load, with the negative half-cycle's current redirected (inverted in direction, not blocked) rather than simply discarded. The result is a pulsed, all-positive output with NO zero-output interval — the "humps" appear twice per input cycle instead of once.

### Comparing the two

- **Average (DC) output**: full-wave rectification gives roughly TWICE the average output of half-wave rectification for the same peak input voltage, because it uses both half-cycles instead of discarding one.
- **Ripple frequency**: half-wave rectification produces one pulse per input cycle (ripple at the fundamental frequency, e.g. 50 Hz); full-wave rectification produces two pulses per input cycle (ripple at TWICE the fundamental frequency, e.g. 100 Hz) — a higher ripple frequency that, as F3.M5.L3 will show, is easier to smooth with a given smoothing capacitor.`,technical:`**Why full-wave rectification is almost always preferred in practice.** Beyond the twice-as-high average output, full-wave rectification's higher ripple frequency means a smoothing capacitor of a given size produces LESS residual ripple voltage (F3.M5.L3) than the same capacitor would with half-wave rectification's lower ripple frequency — a double benefit that makes half-wave rectification rare in anything beyond the simplest, lowest-power applications.

**Diode forward voltage drop's effect on output.** A real diode's forward voltage drop (silicon: typically around 0.6–0.7 V, as measured in F5.M2.L1's diode test) subtracts directly from the rectified output at every conducting instant — a detail that matters more at low output voltages (where 0.7 V is a significant fraction of the total) than at high output voltages.`,diagrams:[{id:`quasi-square`,caption:`Half-wave rectified output (one pulse per cycle, with a zero interval) compared with full-wave rectified output (two pulses per cycle, no zero interval).`}],formulas:[{name:`Diode forward drop (recap, F5.M2.L1)`,expr:`V_out ≈ V_source − V_F`,where:[[`V_F`,`diode forward voltage drop`,`V (≈0.6–0.7 V silicon)`]],note:`Each conducting diode subtracts its own forward drop from the rectified output at every conducting instant.`}],examples:[{title:`Diode conduction direction in half-wave rectification`,problem:`An AC source's instantaneous voltage is +10 V (forward-biasing a series diode) then −10 V (reverse-biasing it) half a cycle later. Describe the load current in each half-cycle.`,steps:[`At +10 V: diode is forward-biased, conducts, current flows to the load (reduced slightly by the diode's forward voltage drop)`,`At −10 V: diode is reverse-biased, blocks almost all current, load current is essentially zero`],answer:`Current flows only during the positive half-cycle (for this diode orientation), giving the pulsed, half-wave output with a zero interval each cycle.`},{title:`Ripple frequency comparison`,problem:`For a 60 Hz AC input, state the ripple frequency produced by (a) half-wave and (b) full-wave rectification.`,steps:[`Half-wave: one pulse per input cycle → ripple at the fundamental, 60 Hz`,`Full-wave: two pulses per input cycle (one per half-cycle) → ripple at twice the fundamental, 120 Hz`],answer:`(a) 60 Hz; (b) 120 Hz.`,check:`Full-wave ripple frequency is always exactly double the input frequency, consistent with using both half-cycles.`}],applications:`This is directly relevant to the rectifier stage inside a battery charger or any AC-to-DC power supply, and to the Build Your Own Inverter laboratory's own treatment of rectification as the "reverse direction" companion to the inverter projects.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming half-wave and full-wave rectifiers produce the same average output for the same input.`,correction:`Full-wave rectification gives roughly double the average output of half-wave for the same peak input, since it uses both half-cycles.`},{mistake:`Assuming rectified output is smooth DC directly from the diodes alone.`,correction:`Diode rectification alone produces a pulsed, ripple-containing output; smoothing (F3.M5.L3) is a separate following stage.`},{mistake:`Ignoring the diode's forward voltage drop when estimating output voltage.`,correction:`Each conducting diode subtracts its forward voltage drop (silicon: ~0.6–0.7 V) from the output at every conducting instant.`}],demo:{title:`Confirm diode behaviour directly`,text:`Use the DMM's diode test mode to confirm forward conduction and reverse blocking on a real diode, the exact behaviour behind rectification.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the DMM`,`dmm`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Digital multimeter`,`dmm`),e.lib(`Diode`,`diode`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Half-wave rectification uses...`,options:[`only one half of the AC input cycle`,`both halves of the cycle`,`no diodes at all`,`only the negative half always`],answer:0,explain:`The diode blocks the other half-cycle entirely.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Compared with half-wave, full-wave rectification's average output for the same peak input is roughly...`,options:[`double`,`the same`,`half`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`Both half-cycles contribute current instead of one.`},{id:`k3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple frequency from full-wave rectification of a 50 Hz input (Hz)?`,answer:100,tol:.01,unit:`Hz`,explain:`Full-wave gives two pulses per cycle: 2×50 = 100 Hz.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A diode's forward voltage drop...`,options:[`subtracts from the rectified output at every conducting instant`,`adds to the output voltage`,`only matters at high frequency`,`is always exactly zero`],answer:0,explain:`A real diode has a small but non-zero forward drop.`}],summary:[`A diode's one-way conduction is the basis of rectification: half-wave rectification uses one diode and one half of each AC cycle.`,`Full-wave rectification uses both halves of the cycle, roughly doubling average output for the same peak input.`,`Full-wave ripple frequency is twice the input frequency; half-wave ripple frequency equals the input frequency.`,`A diode's forward voltage drop subtracts from the rectified output at every conducting instant.`],homework:{id:`F3.M5.L1.hw`,title:`Rectification comparison`,brief:`Compare half-wave and full-wave rectification.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple frequency from full-wave rectification of a 60 Hz input (Hz)?`,answer:120,tol:.01,unit:`Hz`,explain:`2×60 = 120 Hz.`}],written:[{prompt:`A low-cost nightlight circuit uses a single diode (half-wave rectification) to power a small DC LED from AC mains, while a battery charger uses a full-wave bridge. Suggest why the designers might have made these different choices for these two applications.`,model:`The nightlight's LED load is small, cost-sensitive, and tolerant of the lower average output and more pronounced ripple of half-wave rectification — a single diode minimises component count and cost, which matters more than efficiency or smoothness for this simple application. A battery charger, by contrast, needs to deliver a specific, well-controlled average charging current/voltage efficiently, where half-wave rectification's lower average output and harder-to-smooth lower-frequency ripple would be a real disadvantage — the extra diodes in a full-wave bridge are a worthwhile cost for the better output quality and efficiency this application needs.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M5.L2`,title:`The bridge rectifier`,minutes:25,content:{objectives:[`Describe the bridge rectifier topology and trace current flow for each half-cycle.`,`Explain why a bridge rectifier does not require a centre-tapped transformer secondary.`,`State the component tester's relevance to verifying bridge rectifier diodes.`],introduction:`The most common way to achieve full-wave rectification (F3.M5.L1) without needing a centre-tapped transformer secondary is the four-diode **bridge rectifier** — a specific, elegant arrangement worth tracing through carefully.`,lecture:`### The bridge arrangement

Four diodes are arranged so that, for EITHER polarity of the AC input, exactly two of the four diodes conduct and direct current through the load in the SAME direction, while the other two block. As the AC input reverses polarity each half-cycle, the PAIR of conducting diodes swaps, but the current direction through the load stays the same — this is what produces a full-wave, all-one-polarity output from a single, non-tapped AC source.

### Tracing current for each half-cycle

- **Positive half-cycle** (source terminal A positive relative to B): current flows from A, through one diode, through the load (in a fixed direction), through a second diode, and back to B.
- **Negative half-cycle** (B now positive relative to A): current flows from B, through a DIFFERENT diode, through the load (in the SAME fixed direction as before), through the fourth diode, and back to A.

In both cases, the load sees current in the same direction — this swapping of which diode pair conducts, while keeping load current direction fixed, is the key trick of the bridge arrangement.

### Why no centre tap is needed

The alternative full-wave method (F3.M5.L1) uses a centre-tapped secondary with two diodes, exploiting the two equal half-voltages on either side of the centre tap (F3.M4.L4). The bridge rectifier achieves the same full-wave result using FOUR diodes and an ORDINARY (non-centre-tapped) secondary — a simpler transformer at the cost of two extra diodes, which is why the bridge rectifier is the far more common choice in practice, since diodes are cheap and a non-tapped transformer secondary is simpler to wind.

### Checking bridge rectifier diodes

Each diode in a bridge rectifier can be checked individually with the DMM's diode test mode (F5.M2.L1) or evaluated with the component tester's diode procedure (F5.M4's component-tester coverage) — a failed diode in a bridge (open or shorted) causes a characteristic half-wave-like or short-circuit-like fault in the bridge's output, which is a common real-world fault-finding scenario.`,technical:`**Bridge rectifier voltage drop.** Because TWO diodes conduct in series at any instant (rather than one, as in the simpler full-wave centre-tap method), a bridge rectifier's output is reduced by roughly TWICE a single diode's forward voltage drop (around 1.2–1.4 V for silicon diodes) rather than one drop — a detail that matters more at low output voltages.

**Integrated bridge rectifier packages.** In practice, bridge rectifiers are very commonly sold as a single four-terminal packaged component (two AC input terminals, two DC output terminals) rather than built from four discrete diodes — the LCR/component-tester and DMM diode-test principles still apply to each internal diode, though direct individual access may not be possible on a packaged bridge.`,diagrams:[{id:`quasi-square`,caption:`A bridge rectifier's four diodes, with current paths traced for the positive and negative AC half-cycles, both producing the same load current direction.`}],formulas:[{name:`Bridge rectifier peak output (silicon)`,expr:`V_out,pk ≈ V_in,pk − 2·V_F`,where:[[`V_in,pk`,`peak of the AC input to the bridge`,`V`],[`V_F`,`single diode forward drop`,`V (≈0.6–0.7 V)`]],note:`Two diodes conduct in series at any instant, so the combined drop is roughly double a single diode's.`}],examples:[{title:`Diagnosing a bridge rectifier fault from its output`,problem:`A bridge rectifier's output, viewed on an oscilloscope, shows only ONE pulse per input AC cycle instead of the expected two (half-wave-like behaviour from a circuit built as a full bridge). Suggest a plausible fault.`,steps:[`Normal full-wave bridge output has two pulses per cycle, from alternating diode pairs conducting each half-cycle`,`A single-pulse-per-cycle output suggests only ONE half-cycle is actually reaching the load — consistent with one diode (or diode pair) having failed open, so current only flows during the half-cycle that does not need the failed component`],answer:`A plausible fault is one failed-open diode in the bridge, causing the circuit to behave like a half-wave rectifier for one half-cycle while the other half-cycle's path is broken.`},{title:`Why bridge output voltage is slightly lower than expected`,problem:`A bridge rectifier fed from a transformer secondary with 12 V rms is measured to give a peak output noticeably less than 12×√2 = 16.97 V. Explain, using diode conduction, why this is expected.`,steps:[`Two diodes conduct in series at any instant in a bridge rectifier`,`Each diode drops roughly 0.6–0.7 V, so the pair drops roughly 1.2–1.4 V total`],answer:`The expected peak output is reduced by roughly 1.2–1.4 V from the theoretical 16.97 V peak, due to the combined forward voltage drop of the two series-conducting diodes — consistent with a lower-than-theoretical measured peak.`}],applications:`This is the rectifier topology used in essentially every mains-to-DC power supply and battery charger you will encounter in this programme and in practice, and is the stage the oscilloscope and component tester are most often used together to fault-find.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a bridge rectifier needs a centre-tapped transformer.`,correction:`A bridge rectifier specifically avoids needing a centre tap, using four diodes with an ordinary secondary instead.`},{mistake:`Forgetting that TWO diodes conduct in series in a bridge, doubling the voltage drop.`,correction:`Account for roughly 1.2–1.4 V total drop (two diodes in series) when estimating a silicon bridge rectifier's output, not just one diode's drop.`},{mistake:`Assuming all four bridge diodes conduct simultaneously at any instant.`,correction:`Exactly two of the four conduct at a time (the pair appropriate to the current half-cycle's polarity); the other two block.`}],demo:{title:`Test bridge rectifier diodes`,text:`Use the component tester's diode procedure (or the DMM's diode mode) to check each diode in a bridge arrangement individually.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the component tester`,`component-tester`)},activities:[e.instrument(`Component tester`,`component-tester`),e.instrument(`Digital multimeter`,`dmm`),e.sim(`Bridge rectifier experiment`,`sim-03`),e.ckt(`Bridge rectifier exercise`,`ckt-04`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In a bridge rectifier, how many diodes conduct at any one instant?`,options:[`two`,`one`,`four`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`One pair conducts per half-cycle; the other pair blocks.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A key advantage of the bridge rectifier over the centre-tapped full-wave method is...`,options:[`it does not require a centre-tapped transformer secondary`,`it uses fewer diodes`,`it produces half-wave output`,`it eliminates ripple entirely`],answer:0,explain:`It trades a simpler transformer for two extra diodes.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A bridge rectifier's output voltage is reduced by roughly...`,options:[`two diode forward-voltage drops (since two diodes conduct in series)`,`one diode forward-voltage drop`,`zero, ideally`,`four diode drops`],answer:0,explain:`Two diodes are in the conducting path at any instant.`}],summary:[`A bridge rectifier uses four diodes arranged so that each AC half-cycle is directed through the load in the same fixed direction, via a swapping pair of conducting diodes.`,`A bridge rectifier does not require a centre-tapped transformer secondary, unlike the alternative full-wave method.`,`Because two diodes conduct in series at any instant, bridge output voltage is reduced by roughly two diode forward-voltage drops.`,`The DMM's diode test and the component tester can each verify individual bridge rectifier diodes, aiding fault diagnosis.`],homework:{id:`F3.M5.L2.hw`,title:`Bridge rectifier reasoning`,brief:`Reason about bridge rectifier operation and faults.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Expected peak bridge output for a 9 V rms secondary (V_in,pk = 9×√2 = 12.73 V), with two silicon diodes dropping 0.65 V each (V, to 2 decimals)?`,answer:11.43,tol:.03,unit:`V`,explain:`V_out,pk ≈ 12.73 − 2×0.65 = 11.43 V.`}],written:[{prompt:`A bridge rectifier's DC output is measured to be a steady, non-zero voltage, but an oscilloscope shows a flat line with no ripple at all — unusually smooth even for a well-smoothed supply. Suggest what this might indicate, and how you would investigate further.`,model:`A completely flat, ripple-free DC output could indicate either an exceptionally well-designed smoothing stage, or more likely for a simple circuit, a measurement or wiring issue — such as the oscilloscope probe actually measuring a point after an unusually large smoothing capacitor that has essentially become a constant voltage source (if the load is very light), or a fault bypassing the rectifier entirely (such as a shorted diode providing a low-impedance path that happens to look steady). To investigate, I would check the circuit's smoothing capacitor value and load current against the expected ripple using this module's formulas, verify each bridge diode individually with the DMM's diode test or the component tester, and confirm the probe is actually connected at the intended measurement point.`}]}}},{id:`F3.M5.L3`,title:`Smoothing and ripple`,minutes:30,content:{objectives:[`Explain how a smoothing (reservoir) capacitor reduces ripple after rectification.`,`Estimate ripple voltage from load current, capacitance and ripple frequency.`,`Explain why full-wave rectification produces less ripple than half-wave for the same smoothing capacitor and load.`,`Relate smoothing capacitor selection to the ESR/capacitance measurements of F5.M4.L2.`],introduction:`A rectified but unsmoothed output is still a pulsing, far-from-steady voltage. A smoothing (reservoir) capacitor placed across the rectifier's output is what turns those pulses into a usable, nearly-constant DC voltage — and this closing lesson quantifies exactly how well it does so.`,lecture:`### How a smoothing capacitor works

A large capacitor placed across the rectifier output charges up toward the peak of each rectified pulse, then — because the capacitor's reactance at the ripple frequency is far lower than the load's resistance at the DC level (F3.M2.L1's reactance ideas applied directly) — it supplies the load's current itself during the brief interval BETWEEN pulses, when the rectifier's own output would otherwise dip toward zero. The capacitor's voltage sags slightly during this interval, then is topped back up at the next pulse — this residual sag is the **ripple voltage**.

### Estimating ripple voltage

A widely used approximation treats the capacitor as discharging into the load at a roughly constant current I_load during the time T between successive pulses:

**ΔV_ripple ≈ I_load / (f_ripple · C)**

where f_ripple is the ripple frequency (F3.M5.L1: equal to the input frequency for half-wave, or double it for full-wave). This approximation improves as the ripple voltage becomes small relative to the DC level (the usual, well-designed case).

### Why full-wave rectification smooths more easily

For the SAME capacitor and load current, full-wave rectification's ripple frequency is DOUBLE that of half-wave (F3.M5.L1), and the formula above shows ripple voltage is INVERSELY proportional to ripple frequency — so full-wave rectification gives roughly HALF the ripple voltage of half-wave, for the same capacitor and load. This is the second half of the "double benefit" flagged in F3.M5.L1's technical note.

### Choosing a smoothing capacitor

A larger capacitance C, for the same load current and ripple frequency, gives proportionally lower ripple voltage — which is why smoothing capacitors in rectifier/charger circuits are often large electrolytic types, and why their HEALTH (capacitance and ESR, exactly as measured by the LCR meter in F5.M4.L2) matters directly: a capacitor that has dried out and lost capacitance will show noticeably increased ripple on the DC output, often the first symptom of ageing in an electronic power supply.`,technical:`**ESR's additional contribution to ripple.** Beyond the capacitance-based ripple estimate above, a capacitor's own ESR (F5.M4.L2) adds a further small ripple component, because the ripple current flowing through the capacitor also develops a small voltage across its internal ESR. For a healthy capacitor with low ESR this is a minor addition; for an aged capacitor with elevated ESR, this extra ripple contribution can become significant — another reason ESR testing is a meaningful health check beyond capacitance alone.

**Ripple and regulation stages.** In practice, many DC supplies follow the rectifier/smoothing-capacitor stage with a further voltage regulation stage (linear or switching) that further reduces ripple and holds the output voltage constant despite input or load variation — a topic beyond this course's scope, but worth knowing this lesson's "smoothed but not perfectly flat" output is often not the final word.`,diagrams:[{id:`rc-curve`,caption:`A smoothing capacitor charging to the peak of each rectified pulse and discharging slightly into the load between pulses, producing a sawtooth-like ripple voltage.`}],formulas:[{name:`Approximate ripple voltage`,expr:`ΔV_ripple ≈ I_load / (f_ripple · C)`,where:[[`ΔV_ripple`,`peak-to-peak ripple voltage`,`V`],[`I_load`,`load current`,`A`],[`f_ripple`,`ripple frequency`,`Hz`],[`C`,`smoothing capacitance`,`F`]]}],examples:[{title:`Ripple voltage for a full-wave supply`,problem:`A full-wave rectified 50 Hz supply (ripple frequency 100 Hz) feeds a 2 A load through a 4700 µF smoothing capacitor. Estimate the ripple voltage.`,steps:[`ΔV ≈ I_load / (f_ripple × C) = 2 / (100 × 4700×10⁻⁶)`,`= 2 / 0.47`],answer:`≈ 4.26 V peak-to-peak ripple.`},{title:`Comparing half-wave and full-wave ripple for the same components`,problem:`For the same 2 A load and 4700 µF capacitor as the previous example, but with half-wave rectification instead (ripple frequency 50 Hz), estimate the ripple voltage and compare.`,steps:[`ΔV ≈ 2 / (50 × 4700×10⁻⁶)`,`= 2 / 0.235`],answer:`≈ 8.51 V peak-to-peak — almost exactly double the full-wave result (4.26 V), confirming the "half the ripple" benefit of full-wave rectification for the same capacitor and load.`,check:`8.51/4.26 ≈ 2.0, matching the expected doubling from halving the ripple frequency.`}],applications:`This is the exact calculation behind sizing the smoothing/reservoir capacitor in a battery charger or any rectified power supply in this programme — and it directly explains why a worn-out electrolytic capacitor (reduced capacitance, elevated ESR — both detectable with F5.M4.L2's LCR meter) is a classic cause of "unusually noisy" or "hum-heavy" DC output in failing equipment.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Assuming a smoothing capacitor produces perfectly flat DC.`,correction:`Some residual ripple always remains; the capacitor reduces it, following ΔV ≈ I_load/(f_ripple×C), rather than eliminating it.`},{mistake:`Forgetting that half-wave and full-wave rectification give different ripple for the same capacitor.`,correction:`Full-wave's higher ripple frequency (2× half-wave) gives roughly half the ripple voltage for the same C and load current.`},{mistake:`Ignoring ESR's contribution to ripple on an aged capacitor.`,correction:`Elevated ESR adds its own ripple contribution beyond the basic capacitance-based estimate, which is why ESR testing matters for diagnosing ripple problems.`}],demo:{title:`Check a smoothing capacitor's health`,text:`Use the LCR meter's capacitance and ESR readings to assess whether a smoothing capacitor is likely to be producing excess ripple due to ageing.`,link:e.instrument(`Open the LCR meter`,`lcr`)},activities:[e.instrument(`LCR meter`,`lcr`),e.instrument(`Oscilloscope`,`scope`),e.sim(`DC filtering experiment`,`sim-04`),e.ckt(`DC filtering exercise`,`ckt-05`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple voltage for I=1 A, f=100 Hz, C=2200 µF (V, to 2 decimals)?`,answer:4.55,tol:.03,unit:`V`,explain:`ΔV=1/(100×2200e-6)=1/0.22≈4.55 V.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`For the same capacitor and load current, full-wave rectification's ripple voltage compared with half-wave is roughly...`,options:[`half`,`double`,`the same`,`four times larger`],answer:0,explain:`Double the ripple frequency halves the ripple voltage.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A larger smoothing capacitance, for the same load and ripple frequency, gives...`,options:[`proportionally lower ripple voltage`,`proportionally higher ripple voltage`,`no change in ripple`,`only a change in ripple frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Ripple voltage is inversely proportional to capacitance.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An aged capacitor with reduced capacitance and elevated ESR, used for smoothing, typically produces...`,options:[`increased ripple voltage`,`decreased ripple voltage`,`no change in ripple`,`a higher ripple frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Lower C and higher ESR both tend to increase ripple.`}],summary:[`A smoothing capacitor charges to each rectified pulse's peak and supplies load current between pulses, producing a residual ripple voltage.`,`ΔV_ripple ≈ I_load/(f_ripple×C): ripple falls with higher ripple frequency or larger capacitance.`,`Full-wave rectification's double ripple frequency gives roughly half the ripple voltage of half-wave, for the same capacitor and load.`,`A capacitor's ESR adds a further ripple contribution; elevated ESR from ageing (detectable with the LCR meter) is a classic cause of excess ripple in failing equipment.`],homework:{id:`F3.M5.L3.hw`,title:`Ripple calculation practice`,brief:`Estimate ripple voltage for given parameters.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple voltage for I=3 A, f=120 Hz, C=4700 µF (V, to 2 decimals)?`,answer:5.32,tol:.03,unit:`V`,explain:`ΔV=3/(120×4700e-6)=3/0.564≈5.32 V.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Required capacitance for ΔV=2 V, I=1.5 A, f=100 Hz (µF, to the nearest whole number)?`,answer:7500,tol:.03,unit:`µF`,explain:`C=I/(f×ΔV)=1.5/(100×2)=0.0075 F=7500 µF.`}],written:[{prompt:`A piece of equipment that previously ran quietly develops an audible 100 Hz hum from its power supply after several years of service, with no other changes to the circuit or load. Using this lesson's formula, explain a plausible cause and how you would confirm it.`,model:`An audible 100 Hz hum (the full-wave ripple frequency for a 50 Hz supply) appearing after years of service, with no change in load, suggests the smoothing capacitor's effective capacitance has fallen (electrolytic capacitors commonly dry out and lose capacitance with age) — since ΔV_ripple ≈ I_load/(f_ripple×C), a lower C directly increases ripple voltage for the same load current and frequency, and that increased ripple can become audible through a power supply's downstream circuitry. I would confirm this by measuring the capacitor's capacitance and ESR with the LCR meter and comparing against its rated value and a healthy ESR range; a significant capacitance drop or ESR rise would confirm the capacitor as the cause and the next step would be replacement.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.8,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Half-wave rectification uses...`,options:[`only one half of the AC cycle`,`both halves of the cycle`,`no diodes`,`only negative half always`],answer:0,explain:`The diode blocks the other half-cycle.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple frequency from full-wave rectification of a 50 Hz input (Hz)?`,answer:100,tol:.01,unit:`Hz`,explain:`2×50 = 100 Hz.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A bridge rectifier avoids needing...`,options:[`a centre-tapped transformer secondary`,`any diodes`,`an AC input`,`a load`],answer:0,explain:`Four diodes with an ordinary secondary replace the centre-tap method.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In a bridge rectifier, how many diodes conduct at any instant?`,options:[`two`,`one`,`four`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`One pair per half-cycle.`},{id:`q5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple voltage for I=2 A, f=100 Hz, C=4000 µF (V, to 2 decimals)?`,answer:5,tol:.03,unit:`V`,explain:`ΔV=2/(100×4000e-6)=2/0.4=5 V.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`For the same capacitor and load, full-wave rectification's ripple voltage versus half-wave is roughly...`,options:[`half`,`double`,`the same`,`four times`],answer:0,explain:`Double ripple frequency halves ripple voltage.`}]},practicals:[]}],exam:{pass:.8,minutes:50,attempts:3,cooldownHours:24,questions:[{id:`e1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why is power transmitted at high voltage?`,options:[`To reduce current and resistive loss for the same power`,`Because generators can only make high voltage`,`To make it safer to touch`,`To reduce frequency`],answer:0,explain:`Loss = I²R; lower current at the same power sharply reduces loss.`},{id:`e2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Period of a 50 Hz waveform (ms)?`,answer:20,tol:.01,unit:`ms`,explain:`T = 1/50 = 20 ms.`},{id:`e3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`RMS of a pure sine with 100 V peak (V)?`,answer:70.7,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`100/√2 = 70.7 V.`},{id:`e4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`RMS of an ideal square wave equals...`,options:[`its peak value`,`peak/√2`,`half its peak`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`A square wave is at full amplitude the whole cycle.`},{id:`e5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Unfiltered SPWM, on an oscilloscope, looks like...`,options:[`a fast train of varying-width pulses, not a smooth sine`,`a smooth sine directly`,`a plain square wave`,`a DC level`],answer:0,explain:`Filtering afterward produces the smooth sine.`},{id:`e6`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`X_C of a 100 µF capacitor at 50 Hz (Ω, to 2 decimals)?`,answer:31.83,tol:.02,unit:`Ω`,explain:`X_C = 1/(2π×50×100e-6) ≈ 31.83 Ω.`},{id:`e7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`As frequency rises, inductive reactance...`,options:[`rises`,`falls`,`stays constant`,`becomes resistive`],answer:0,explain:`X_L = 2πfL.`},{id:`e8`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Impedance for R = 30 Ω, X = 40 Ω in series (Ω)?`,answer:50,tol:.01,unit:`Ω`,explain:`√(900+1600)=√2500=50 Ω.`},{id:`e9`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`At resonance, net reactance is...`,options:[`zero`,`maximum`,`infinite`,`equal to R always`],answer:0,explain:`X_L = X_C at resonance.`},{id:`e10`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Apparent power of 230 V rms at 10 A rms (VA)?`,answer:2300,tol:.01,unit:`VA`,explain:`S = 230×10 = 2300 VA.`},{id:`e11`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Power factor for P = 1500 W, S = 2000 VA?`,answer:.75,tol:.01,unit:``,explain:`PF = 1500/2000 = 0.75.`},{id:`e12`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A lagging power factor is caused by...`,options:[`net inductive reactance`,`net capacitive reactance`,`purely resistive loads`,`a fault`],answer:0,explain:`Inductive loads make current lag voltage.`},{id:`e13`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`True power factor differs from displacement power factor by also including...`,options:[`harmonic current distortion`,`supply frequency`,`ambient temperature`,`the meter brand`],answer:0,explain:`True PF accounts for phase shift and harmonic content.`},{id:`e14`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Over a full cycle, an ideal reactive load's net energy transfer is...`,options:[`zero`,`positive`,`negative`,`equal to apparent power`],answer:0,explain:`Energy is stored and returned each cycle.`},{id:`e15`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Secondary voltage for N_p=800, N_s=40, V_p=230 V (V)?`,answer:11.5,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`V_s = 230×(40/800) = 11.5 V.`},{id:`e16`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Copper loss in a transformer winding scales with...`,options:[`the square of load current`,`load current linearly`,`frequency only`,`core material only`],answer:0,explain:`P = I²R.`},{id:`e17`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Galvanic isolation means...`,options:[`no direct conductive path between primary and secondary`,`a 1:1 voltage ratio`,`no magnetic core`,`no load connected`],answer:0,explain:`Only a magnetic link exists between the windings.`},{id:`e18`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A star winding connection provides a natural point for...`,options:[`a neutral conductor`,`a centre tap`,`core lamination`,`leakage reactance`],answer:0,explain:`The common junction of the three windings.`},{id:`e19`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple frequency from full-wave rectification of a 50 Hz input (Hz)?`,answer:100,tol:.01,unit:`Hz`,explain:`Full-wave gives two pulses per cycle: 2×50=100 Hz.`},{id:`e20`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In a bridge rectifier, how many diodes conduct at any one instant?`,options:[`two`,`one`,`four`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`One pair conducts per half-cycle; the other pair blocks.`},{id:`e21`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Ripple voltage for I=2 A, f=100 Hz, C=4000 µF (V, to 2 decimals)?`,answer:5,tol:.03,unit:`V`,explain:`ΔV=2/(100×4000e-6)=2/0.4=5 V.`}]}};export{t as F3};